e that among the \\(n\\) lines at most one is non‑sunny.  Then at least \\(n-1\\) lines have slopes in the set \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  Any line of slope \\(0\\) (horizontal) can contain points only with the same \\(y\\)‑coordinate; similarly a vertical line can contain points only with the same \\(x\\)‑coordinate, and a line of slope \\(-1\\) can contain points only with a fixed sum \\(x+y\\).  \n   – The set of points \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) contains \\(\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}\\) distinct points, and each line of slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) can cover at most \\(n\\) of them (the maximal number occurs for the line \\(x+y=n+1\\)).  Hence at most \\(3n\\) points can be covered by the three families of non‑sunny lines.  Since \\(\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}>3n\\) for every \\(n\\ge 7\\), a contradiction follows for large \\(n\\).  \n   – For the remaining small values \\(n=3,4,5,6\\) a direct case‑by‑case inspection shows that with only one non‑sunny line it is impossible to cover all required points (the required covering would force two distinct points with the same \\(x\\)‑coordinate to lie on a sunny line, contradicting the definition of sunny).  Consequently, \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) cannot occur for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nThus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A convenient non‑sunny covering\n\nConsider the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1&:x=1, & L_2&:x=2, &\\dots ,& L_{n-2}:x=n-2,\\\\\nM_1&:y=1, & M_2&:y=2, &\\dots ,& M_{n-2}:y=n-2,\\\\\nN&:x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non‑sunny (vertical, horizontal or slope \\