 – Each of the \\(t\\) non‑vertical lines can contain at most one point from each of the \\(n\\) columns, hence at most \\(n\\) points in total; together they contribute at most \\(tn\\) points.  \n   – The remaining \\(n-t\\) lines are vertical.  A vertical line \\(x=i\\) can contain at most \\(|C_i|=n+1-i\\) points.  To maximise the total number of points covered by \\(n-t\\) distinct vertical lines we choose the columns with the largest sizes, namely \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-t\\).  Their sizes sum to  \n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{\\,n-t}(n+1-i)=\\frac{(n-t)(n+t+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n   Adding the two contributions yields (1). ∎  \n\n   *Corollary.* If \\(t\\ge 2\\) then the right‑hand side of (1) is **strictly smaller** than \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).  \n   *Proof.* From (1) we have  \n   \\[\n   N_{\\mathcal L}\\le \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\frac{(t-1)t}{2}.\n   \\]\n   For \\(t\\ge2\\) the subtracted term \\(\\frac{(t-1)t}{2}>0\\), so \\(N_{\\mathcal L}<|T_n|\\). ∎  \n\n   Consequently any family of \\(n\\) lines that covers **all** points of \\(T_n\\) can contain **at most one** non‑vertical line.  In other words  \n\n   \\[\n   t\\le 1.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Realisation of the two possibilities.**  \n\n   *Case \\(k=0\\) (no sunny line).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), hence lies on \\(L_a\\).  All lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, so none is sunny.  Thus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n   *Case \\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line).*  \n   Use the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (these cover all columns except the last one) and add a single non‑vertical line that passes through the unique point of the last column, namely \\((n,1)\\).  \n   For example, the line  \n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\; y = x - n + 1\n   \\]\n   has slope \\(1\\); it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\), hence it is sunny.  It contains the point \\((n,1)\\) and no other point of \\(T_n\\).  Together the \\(n\\) lines cover every point of \\(T_n\\), and exactly one of them (\\(\\ell\\)) is sunny.  Hence \\(k=1\\) is also attainable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n4. **Exclusion of larger \\(k\\).**  \n   By the corollary in step 2, a configuration with \\(t\\ge 2\\) non‑vertical (i.e. sunny) lines cannot cover all points of \\(T_n\\).  Therefore \\(k\\ge 2\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nCombining the constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) with the impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\) yields the complete answer.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
