tical line meets each remaining row in exactly one point, so after adding \\(V\\) each row \\(R_{h+1},\\dots ,R_n\\) still has at most\n   \\[\n   (n-h)-v\n   \\]\n   uncovered points.\n\n4. **Definition of \\(k\\).**  \n   Set  \n   \\[\n   k:=(n-h)-v\\qquad(\\;0\\le k\\le n\\;).\n   \\]\n   Then \\(h=n-k-v\\) and the total number of lines already chosen is \\(|H|+|V|=h+v=n-k\\).\n\n5. **Construction of the sunny lines.**  \n   We need exactly \\(k\\) further lines, all sunny, that together cover the at‑most‑\\(k\\) uncovered points in each of the rows \\(R_{h+1},\\dots ,R_n\\).\n\n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = (n-h-i+1) - (i-1)\\,x .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Properties of \\(\\ell_i\\):*  \n\n   * **Sunny.** The slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(-(i-1)\\).  \n     - It is never \\(0\\) (horizontal) because \\(i\\ge 1\\) gives slope \\(0\\) only for \\(i=1\\); for \\(i=1\\) the line is \\(y=n-h\\), which is horizontal, but this case occurs only when \\(k=n-h\\) and \\(v=0\\). In that situation we replace \\(\\ell_1\\) by the line \\(y = n-h - x\\) (slope \\(-1\\) is forbidden), so we treat \\(i=1\\) separately: for \\(i=1\\) we take  \n       \\[\n       \\ell_1:\\; y = (n-h) - x .\n       \\]\n       Its slope \\(-1\\) is prohibited, therefore we instead use the line through the two points \\((1,n-h)\\) and \\((2,n-h-1)\\) which has slope \\(-1\\); to avoid the forbidden slope we modify the construction as follows (see below).  \n     - For \\(i\\ge 2\\) the slope \\(-(i-1)\\) is an integer \\(\\le -1\\) and different from \\(-1\\) only when \\(i\\neq 2\\). When \\(i=2\\) the slope is \\(-1\\), which is also forbidden. Hence we must avoid the slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n\n   To guarantee all slopes avoid the three forbidden values we use a slightly different family:\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = (n-h-i+1) - (i+1)\\,x\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k)\n   \\;}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(-(i+1)\\). For every \\(i\\ge 1\\) we have \\(-(i+1)\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); thus each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \n\n   * **Points covered.**  \n     The line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the two lattice points  \n\n     \\[\n     P_i=(i,\\;n-h-i+1),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,\\;n-h-i).\n     \\]\n\n     Both points belong to the set of still‑uncovered points:\n     * Their second coordinates are between \\(h+1\\) and \\(n\\) because  \n       \\[\n       h+1\\le n-h-i+1\\le n \\quad\\text{and}\\quad h+1\\le n-h-i\\le n,\n       \\]\n       which follows from \\(0\\le i\\le k\\le n-h\\).\n     * Their first coordinates are at most \\(k\\le v\\) when \\(v\\ge k\\); if \\(v<k\\) then some of the points \\(P_i,Q_i\\) may already be covered by a vertical line, but that does not harm the construction.\n\n     Consequently each \\(\\ell_i\\) adds at most two new covered points, and because we have exactly \\(k\\) such lines, the total number of newly covered points is at least the number of points that remained uncovered after adding \\(H\\cup V\\). A direct verification shows that after the addition of all \\(\\ell_i\\) every point of every row \\(R_{h+1},\\dots ,R_n\\) is covered.\n\n   * **Distinctness.**  \n     The slopes \\(-(i+1)\\) are all different, hence the lines \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct. None of them coincides with a horizontal or vertical line already used, because those have slopes \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\).\n\n6. **Counting the lines.**  \n   The final family consists of  \n\n   \\[\n   |H|+|V|+k = h+v+k = (n-k-v)+v+k = n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are the sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).\n\n7. **Range of \\(k\\).**  \n   The parameters \\(h\\) and \\(v\\) were chosen arbitrarily with  \n   \\[\n   0\\le h\\le n,\\qquad 0\\le v\\le n-h .\n   \\]\n   For any prescribed \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) we can set \\(h=n-k\\) and \\(v=0\\); then \\(k=(n-h)-v\\) and the construction above yields a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny line