e negative, \\(k\\ge 0\\).  \nHence any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) satisfying (1)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and prove that they cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  The sunny lines  \n\nChoose any \\(k\\) distinct real numbers  \n\n\\[\nm_1,m_2,\\dots ,m_k\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\n\\]\n\n(the value \\(\\infty\\) is excluded, i.e. we do not take vertical lines).  \nFor each \\(i\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y-1=m_i\\,(x-1) \\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll lines \\(L_i\\) pass through the point \\((1,1)\\) and have slopes \\(m_i\\); consequently each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  The non‑sunny lines  \n\nFor the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines we take vertical lines\n\n\\[\nV_j:\\; x=j+1\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,n-k). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_j\\) has slope \\(\\infty\\) and therefore is non‑sunny.  \nThe lines in (2) and (3) are pairwise distinct because the slopes in (2) are finite and different from each other, while the lines in (3) have a different (vertical) direction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3  Coverage of the set \\(T_n\\)\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  Two cases are possible.\n\n* **Case 1.** \\(a\\ge 2\\).  \n  Then \\(a=j+1\\) for a unique integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-k\\) **iff** \\(j=a-1\\le n-k\\).  \n  Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have  \n\n  \\[\n  a\\le n+1-b\\le n,\n  \\]\n\n  hence \\(a-1\\le n-1\\).  Because \\(n-k\\ge 0\\), the integer \\(j=a-1\\) indeed belongs to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\) whenever \\(a\\ge 2\\).  \n  Consequently the vertical line \