if they\nsatisfy \\(a_i+b_i=a_j+b_j\\) for some \\(i\\neq j\\), the line would have slope\n\\(-1\\).  Since \\(L\\) is sunny, none of these coincidences can occur, so the\nthree points have pairwise distinct \\(x\\), distinct \\(y\\) and distinct sums\n\\(a+b\\).  Three points with all three pairwise differences non‑zero cannot be\ncollinear (the determinant of their homogeneous coordinates is non‑zero).\nThus a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\ns\\le 2k. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nA non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n*Horizontal line* \\(y=c\\) (\\(c\\ge 1\\)): points of \\(T_n\\) on it satisfy\n\\(a+c\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) such points.  \n\n*Vertical line* \\(x=c\\) (\\(c\\ge 1\\)): similarly at most \\(n\\) points.  \n\n*Slope \\(-1\\) line* \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)): admissible points are\n\\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\); again at most \\(n\\) points. ∎\n\n\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nt\\le n\\,(n-k). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Deriving the bound  \n\nInsert (3) and (4) into (2):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=s+t\\le 2k+n(n-k)=n^{2}+2k-nk .\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\nnk\\le n^{2}+2k-\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n      =\\frac{n^{2}}{2}+2k-\\frac{n}{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich yields\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is a non‑negative integer, any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3. Construction attaining every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe now show that for each integer \\(k\\) satisfying (5) there exists a set of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\)\nsunny lines.\n\n#### 3.1. The non‑sunny part  \n\nTake the following \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines:\n\n* **Horizontal lines**  \n  \\[\n  y=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n-k-2\\qquad\\text{(if }n-k\\ge 3\\text{)}.\n  \\]\n  There are \\(n-k-2\\) of them.\n\n* **One vertical line**  \n  \\[\n  x=n-k-1 .\n  \\]\n\n* **One line of slope \\(-1\\)**  \n  \\[\n  x+y=n-k .\n  \\]\n\nAltogether we have \\((n-k-2)+1+1=n-k\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.  \nA straightforward verification shows that every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with\n\\(b\\le n-k-2\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\); the points with\n\\(b=n-k-1\\) lie on the vertical line \\(x=n-k-1\\); and the points with\n\\(a+b=n-k\\) lie on the line \\(x+y=n-k\\).  Thus all points of \\(T_n\\) whose\ncoordinates satisfy either \\(b\\le n-k-2\\) or \\(a+b=n-k\\) are already covered.\n\nThe only points of \\(T_n\\) not yet covered are\n\n\\[\n(1,\\,n-k),\\;(2,\\,n-k-1),\\dots ,\\;(k,\\,n-k+1),\\;(k+1,\\,2).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n(When \\(k=0\\) the set (6) is empty, and the non‑sunny lines already cover\n\\(T_n\\).)\n\n#### 3.2. The sunny part  \n\nFor each point listed in (6) we introduce a sunny line as follows.\n\n*For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\)* define the line \\(L_i\\) through the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\,n-k+2-i),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,\\,2).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{2-(n-k+2-i)}{(i+1)-i}=i-(n-k),\n\\]\n\nwhich is an integer different from \\(0\\) (because \\(i\\le k\\le n-2\\)),\ndifferent from \\(\\infty\\) and different from \\(-1\\) (since \\(i-(n-k)\\neq -1\\)\nfor the allowed range).  Hence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\n*If \\(k\\ge 1\\)* the line \\(L_k\\) already contains the point \\((k+1,2)\\); thus\nthe \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\) cover exactly the points in (6).\n\nAll these sunny lines are distinct because they have distinct slopes\n\\(m_i\\).  Moreover each of them contains precisely the two points of (6) that\nwere assigned to it, and no other point of \\(T_n\\) (by Lemma 1 a sunny line\ncannot contain a third point of \\(T_n\\)).\n\n#### 3.3. Verification  \n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-k-2)+1+1}_{\\text{non‑sunny}}+\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny}}=n .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\