\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nlies on a line of \\(\\mathcal F_0\\).\n\n*Proof check:*  \n\n- If \\(a\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).  \n- If \\(a=n\\) then the inequality forces \\(b=1\\); the point \\((n,1)\\) satisfies \\(n+1=n+1\\) and lies on \\(D\\).\n\n**Verification:** The reasoning is correct; the lemma holds. No issue.\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) is a valid configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3. Replacing a vertical line by a \u201csunny\u201d line\n\nFor a fixed \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-2\\) the solution proposes to replace  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\;x=t\n\\]\n\nby  \n\n\\[\n\\widetilde L_t:\\; y = (n-t-1)(x-t)+1. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n**Step\u202f3.1 \u2013 Sunny property**\n\nThe slope of \\(\\widetilde L_t\\) is \\(n-t-1\\). Since \\(1\\le t\\le n-2\\) and \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\nn-t-1\\notin\\{0,-1\\},\\qquad \\text{and the line is not vertical.}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(\\widetilde L_t\\) is indeed sunny.  \n*Correct.*\n\n**Step\u202f3.2 \u2013 Two lattice points retained**\n\n- Substituting \\(x=t\\) gives \\(y=1\\); thus \\((t,1)\\in\\widetilde L_t\\).  \n- Substituting \\(x=t+1\\) gives \\(y=n-t\\); thus \\((t+1,n-t)\\in\\widetilde L_t\\).\n\nBoth statements are algebraically correct.\n\n**Step\u202f3.3 \u2013 Claim that covering is preserved**\n\nThe solution argues:\n\n1. \\((t,1)\\) is now on \\(\\widetilde L_t\\).  \n2. \\((t+1,n-t)\\) already lies on the diagonal \\(D\\).  \n\nThese two points are indeed covered after the replacement.\n\nThe crucial part is the treatment of **all other points** of the original column \\(L_t\\), i.e. points \\((t,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge2\\) and \\(t+b\\le n+1\\).\n\nThe solution\u2019s argument proceeds as follows:\n\n- From \\(t+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(b\\le n-t\\).  \n- **Case A:** If \\(b=n-t\\) the point coincides with \\((t,n-t)\\), which the solution claims \u201clies on the diagonal \\(D\\)\u201d.  \n- **Case B:** If \\(b<n-t\\) then \\(b\\ge2\\) and the point \\((t,b)\\) also satisfies \\(t+b\\le n\\); consequently the point \\((t+b, n-t-b+1)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D\\).  \n- In either case the point \\((t,b)\\) is said to be \u201calready covered by a line that has not been altered (the diagonal \\(D\\) or another vertical line)\u201d.\n\n**Critical analysis of this argument:**\n\n- **Error in Case\u202fA:** The diagonal \\(D\\) is defined by \\(x+y=n+1\\). For the point \\((t,n-t)\\) we have \\(t+(n-t)=n\\neq n+1\\). Hence \\((t,n-t)\\) **does not** lie on \\(D\\). The correct point of the original column that lies on \\(D\\) is \\((t, n+1-t)\\), i.e. the *top* point of the column, not \\((t,n-t)\\). The solution\u2019s statement is false.\n\n- **Error in Case\u202fB:** The construction of the auxiliary point \\((t+b, n-t-b+1)\\) indeed lies on \\(D\\) because \\((t+b)+(n-t-b+1)=n+1\\). However, this does **not** imply that the original point \\((t,b)\\) is covered by any line after the replacement. The point \\((t,b)\\) is not on \\(D\\) (its sum is \\(t+b\\le n\\)), nor is it on any unchanged vertical line (all vertical lines have equation \\(x=\\text{constant}\\neq t\\)). The solution incorrectly assumes that the existence of a *different* point on \\(D\\) somehow covers \\((t,b)\\). This is a logical fallacy; the covering property is lost for many points of the column.\n\n- **Concrete counter\u2011example:** Take \\(n=4\\) and \\(t=1\\). Then \\(\\widetilde L_1\\) is \\(y=2x-1\\). The original column \\(L_1\\) contains the points \\((1,2)\\) and \\((1,3)\\). After the replacement, \\((1,2)\\) is not on \\(\\widetilde L_1\\) (since \\(2\\neq2\\cdot1-1=1\\)) and is not on any other line of \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) (the diagonal is \\(x+y=5\\), which does not contain \\((1,2)\\)). Hence \\((1,2)\\) becomes uncovered, contradicting the claim that the covering is preserved.\n\nBecause the covering argument fails, **the entire construction of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) collapses**. This is a **Critical Error** that invalidates the subsequent steps that rely on the preservation of