            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*The point \\((n,1)\\).*  \nSince \\(n+(1)=n+1\\), the only line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) that can contain \\((n,1)\\) is the line\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=n+1,\n\\]\nbecause any line with a different slope would intersect the vertical line \\(x=n\\) at a point whose \\(y\\)-coordinate is not \\(1\\).  \nThus \\(D\\in\\mathcal L\\). Its slope is \\(-1\\); consequently \\(D\\) is **non‑sunny**.\n\n*The column \\(x=n-1\\).*  \nThe points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with abscissa \\(n-1\\) are \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\).  \nAny line that contains both of them is vertical, i.e. has slope \\(\\infty\\); therefore it is non‑sunny.  \nConsequently the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_{n-1}:\\;x=n-1\n\\]\n\nmust belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\) (otherwise one of the two points would be uncovered).  \n\nThus every admissible family contains at least the two non‑sunny lines \\(D\\) and \\(V_{n-1}\\).  \nHence the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A covering with no sunny line (\\(k=0\\))\n\nConsider  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_0=\\{V_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\cup\\{D\\},\n\\]\nwhere \\(V_i:x=i\\) and \\(D:x+y=n+1\\).\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\). Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b\\ge1\\), we have \\(a\\le n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).  \n* If \\(a=n\\) then necessarily \\(b=1\\); the point \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(D\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered.  \nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) are non‑sunny (vertical or slope \\(-1\\)), so \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) realises \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Replacing a vertical line by a sunny line\n\nFix an integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-2\\).  \nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_t:\\; y=(n-t-1)(x-t)+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n**Lemma 3.1.** The line \\(\\ell_t\\) is sunny, passes through \\((t,1)\\) and \\((t+1,n-t)\\), and after replacing \\(V_t\\) by \\(\\ell_t\\) the family still covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe slope of \\(\\ell_t\\) equals \\(n-t-1\\). Because \\(1\\le t\\le n-2\\) and \\(n\\ge3\\), we have \\(n-t-1\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and the line is not vertical; hence \\(\\ell_t\\) is sunny.\n\nSubstituting \\(x=t\\) in (2) gives \\(y=1\\); substituting \\(x=t+1\\) gives \\(y=n-t\\). Thus \\(\\ell_t\\) contains the two lattice points \\((t,1)\\) and \\((t+1,n-t)\\).\n\nThe point \\((t+1,n-t)\\) also satisfies \\((t+1)+(n-t)=n+1\\); therefore it lies on the diagonal \\(D\\). Consequently it remains covered after the replacement.\n\nConsider any other point of the original column \\(V_t\\); it has the form \\((t,b)\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-t\\) (the inequality follows from \\(t+b\\le n+1\\)).  \n\n* If \\(b=n-t\\) then \\((t,b)\\) lies on \\(D\\) and is still covered.  \n* If \\(b<n-t\\) then \\(b\\ge2\\) and the point \\((b,1)\\) belongs to the vertical line \\(V_b\\), which has not been altered. Hence \\((t,b)\\) is covered by the unchanged line \\(V_b\\).\n\nAll other points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are untouched. Therefore the new family (the original family with \\(V_t\\) replaced by \\(\\ell_t\\)) still covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Obtaining any number \\(k\\) of sunny lines with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nChoose any subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) with \\(|S|=k\\).  \nStarting from \\(\\mathcal F_0\\), replace each vertical line \\(V_t\\) with \\(t\\in S\\) by the sunny line \\(\\ell_t\\) given by (2).  \n\nDenote the resulting family by \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).  \nBy Lemma 3.1 each replacement preserves the covering property and introduces exactly one sunny line.  \nSince the slopes \\(n-t-1\\) are distinct for different \\(t\\), all newly created lines are distinct from one another and from the unchanged lines.  \n\nThus \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), and contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\)\n\nAs shown in Section 1, any admissible family must contain the two non‑sunny lines \\(D\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) and \\(V_{n-1}\\) (vertical).  \nConsequently the number of sunny lines can never reach \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\).  \nTogether with the construction of Section 4 this yields the exact set of attainable values.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Conclusion\n\nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the integers from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-2\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All non‑negative integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le