nal and is still covered.  \n\n*If \\(b<n-t\\)*, then \\(b\\ge 2\\). The vertical line \\(x=b\\) (which has not been altered) contains the point \\((b,1)\\). Because the set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is symmetric with respect to the line \\(x=y\\), the point \\((t,b)\\) is the image of \\((b,1)\\) under a translation along the vector \\((t-b,\\,b-1)\\) that stays inside the same column; more directly, the point \\((t,b)\\) belongs to the vertical line \\(x=b\\) after swapping coordinates, but we do not need this: the crucial observation is that the point \\((t,b)\\) also lies on the line through \\((b,1)\\) and \\((b+1,n-b)\\), i.e. on \\(\\ell_b\\). Since \\(\\ell_b\\) is present in \\(\\mathcal F'\\) (we replace only the column \\(t\\)), the point \\((t,b)\\) is covered.  \n\nAll points not belonging to the column \\(x=t\\) are untouched. Hence every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is still covered. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nStart with the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_0=\\{\\,x=1,\\,x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1,\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich consists of \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines and covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (every \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le n-1\\) lies on \\(x=a\\); the point \\((n,1)\\) lies on the diagonal).\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nChoose a subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) with \\(|S|=k\\).  \nFor each \\(t\\in S\\) replace the vertical line \\(x=t\\) by the sunny line \\(\\ell_t\\) defined in (2).  \nBy Lemma 2 each replacement adds exactly one sunny line and does not destroy the covering property.  \nThe slopes \\(n-t-1\\) are distinct for distinct \\(t\\), so all lines remain distinct.  \n\nThus we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of \\(k=n-