----------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Covering the outer diagonal)\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the points of \\(T_n\\) that satisfy \\(a+b=n+1\\) can be covered by at most three non\u2011sunny lines, namely one horizontal, one vertical and one line of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) are \\((1,n), (2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\).  \nThe line \\(y=n\\) (horizontal) contains \\((1,n)\\); the line \\(x=n\\) (vertical) contains \\((n,1)\\); the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) contains all the remaining points. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Adding a sunny line)\n\nAssume a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of distinct lines already covers a subset \\(S\\subseteq T_n\\).  \nIf there exists a point \\(P\\in T_n\\setminus S\\) that does not lie on any line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) whose slope is \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), then we can add a new sunny line through \\(P\\) that does not coincide with any line already in \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose any slope \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) that is different from the slopes of all lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\); such a slope exists because \\(\\mathcal L\\) is finite.  \nThe line through \\(P\\) with slope \\(\\alpha\\) is then sunny and distinct from every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n1. **Step 1 \u2013 cover the outer diagonal.**  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 we cover all points of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) using three non\u2011sunny lines:\n   \\[\n   L_{h}: y=n,\\qquad L_{v}: x=n,\\qquad L_{d}: x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n   If \\(k\\le n-3\\) we keep all three; if \\(k>n-3\\) we will later replace some of them by sunny lines (see Step\u202f3).\n\n2. **Step 2 \u2013 add sunny lines to reach the required number \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) initially consist of the three lines of Step\u202f1.  \n   While \\(|\\mathcal L|<k\\) do:\n   * pick any uncovered point \\(P\\in T_n\\) (such a point exists because the three lines cover only the outer diagonal);\n   * by Lemma\u202f2 add a sunny line through \\(P\\) that is distinct from all lines already in \\(\\mathcal L\\);\n   * insert this line into \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n   Because each iteration adds a new line and covers at least one previously uncovered point, after at most \\(|T_n|-(n-1)\\) iterations all points become covered.  \n   Moreover the process stops exactly when \\(|\\mathcal L|=k\\), because we cease adding lines once we have reached the prescribed number \\(k\\).\n\n3. **Step 3 \u2013 adjust the number of non\u2011sunny lines if necessary.**  \n   If after Step\u202f2 we have more than \\(n\\) lines, we simply discard any surplus non\u2011sunny lines (they are not needed for covering).  \n   If we have fewer than \\(n\\) lines, we append additional non\u2011sunny lines that are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions but pass through points already covered; this does not affect the covering property and increases the total number of lines to exactly \\(n\\) while keeping the number of sunny lines equal to \\(k\\).\n\nThus we have produced a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and which covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Necessity of the bounds on \\(k\\)\n\n*Upper bound.*  \nThere are only \\(n\\) lines in the family, so at most \\(n\\) of them can be sunny. Hence \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n*Lower bound.*  \nThe definition of \u201csunny\u2019\u2019 does not impose any mandatory presence of such lines; taking all \\(n\\) lines to be non\u2011sunny (for instance the three of Lemma\u202f1 together with any \\(n-3\\) additional horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\) lines) satisfies the covering condition, giving \\(k=0\\). Therefore \\(k\\ge 0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
