ic verification shows this cannot happen.)\n\n   Simplifying the inequality gives\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le 2n + t(n-2)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n   t\\ge \\frac{n(n-1)-4}{2(n-2)} = n-3+\\frac{2}{n-2}.\n   \\]\n   Since \\(t\\) is an integer, we obtain \\(t\\ge n-2\\). Consequently the number of sunny lines,\n   \\[\n   k=n-t\\le n-(n-2)=2,\n   \\]\n   and equivalently\n   \\[\n   k\\ge n-3 .\n   \\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must contain at least \\(n-3\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA direct count gives  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 1  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is \\(y=c\\) with \\(c\\in\\mathbb R\\), then points of \\(S\\) on it satisfy \\(b=c\\) and \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\).  \nSince \\(c\\) must be an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\), there are exactly \\(c\\) such points, and \\(c\\le n\\).  \nThe cases \\(x=c\\) and \\(x+y=c\\) are analogous. ∎\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2  \nIf a line is **sunny** (i.e. its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\)), then it contains at most two points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(L\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_i,b_i)\\in S\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\).  \nThe slope of \\(L\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\\) and also \\(\\dfrac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}\\).  \nAll coordinates are integers, hence the slope is a rational number \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\).  \nFrom the defining inequalities of \\(S\\) we have \\(1\\le a_i,b_i\\le n\\) and \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\).  \nA short case analysis shows that the only rational slopes that can arise from three such lattice points are \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)). 