c\\) are analogous. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Necessity\n\nAssume we are given \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S\\).  \nLet \\(t\\) be the number of non\u2011sunny lines (i.e. lines parallel to one of the three forbidden directions) and \\(k=n-t\\) the number of sunny lines.\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 each non\u2011sunny line covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\); therefore the \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines together cover at most \\(tn\\) points.  \nConsequently the remaining \\(k\\) sunny lines must cover at least  \n\n\\[\n|S|-tn = \\frac{n(n+1)}2 - tn\n\\]\n\npoints.  Since a line can always be drawn through any single point, there is no obstruction to covering the remaining points with \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.  In particular the inequality  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le tn + k\\cdot n = n^{2}\n\\]\n\nholds for every \\(t\\) (because \\(tn\\le n^{2}\\)).  Thus **no numerical restriction** on \\(t\\) (and hence on \\(k\\)) follows from the covering requirement.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Sufficiency \u2013 construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put \\(t=n-k\\).\n\n1. **Choose the non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Take the horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{i}:\\; y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,t).\n   \\]\n\n   These \\(t\\) lines are distinct, non\u2011sunny, and by Lemma\u202f1 each contains exactly \\(i\\) points of \\(S\\).  \n   Hence the total number of points of \\(S\\) already covered is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{t} i = \\frac{t(t+1)}2 .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Identify the uncovered points.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   U:=S\\setminus\\bigcup_{i=1}^{t}L_{i}.\n   \\]\n\n   From (1) and (2),\n\n   \\[\n   |U| = \\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{t(t+1)}2\n        = \\frac{(n-t)(n+t+1)}2 = \\frac{k(2n-k+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   In particular \\(|U|\\le k\\) when \\(k\\le 2\\); for larger \\(k\\) we will