            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=\\text{constant}\\)).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Necessary condition\n\nBecause we are required to produce exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Three non‑sunny lines covering the outer diagonal\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_h:\\;y=n,\\qquad L_v:\\;x=n,\\qquad L_d:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nAll three are non‑sunny.  \n\n**Lemma 1.** Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lies on at least one of \\(L_h,L_v,L_d\\).\n\n*Proof.* The points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) are \\((1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\).  \n\\((1,n)\\) belongs to \\(L_h\\); \\((n,1)\\) belongs to \\(L_v\\); every other point satisfies \\(x+y=n+1\\) and therefore lies on \\(L_d\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Covering the interior points with non‑sunny lines\n\nThe interior of \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\nI_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsider the following set of non‑sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N=\n\\underbrace{\\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-3\\}}_{\\text{vertical lines}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\{y=1,\\;y=2\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal N\\) are non‑sunny, they are distinct, and \\(|\\mathcal N|= (n-3)+2 = n-1\\).\n\n**Lemma 2.** Every point of \\(I_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal N\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in I_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-3\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which belongs to \\(\\mathcal N\\)) contains the point.\n\n* If \\(a=n-2\\) then \\(b\\le2\\) because \\(a+b\\le n\\); hence the point lies on \\(y=1\\) or \\(y=2\\).\n\n* If \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le1\\); thus the point lies on \\(y=1\\).\n\n* The case \\(a=n\\) cannot occur because \\(a+b\\le n\\) would force \\(b\\le0\\), contradicting \\(b\\ge1\\).\n\nThus in all cases the point belongs to a line of \\(\\mathcal N\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Adding exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe start from the three non‑sunny lines \\(L_h,L_v,L_d\\) and the set \\(\\mathcal N\\) of Lemma 2.\n\nIf \\(k=0\\) we are done: the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_0 = \\{L_h,L_v,L_d\\}\\cup\\mathcal N\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(3+(n-1)=n+2\\) lines.  Deleting any two of the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) leaves exactly \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines, and Lemma 1 together with Lemma 2 guarantees that every point of \\(T_n\\) is still covered.  Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\nAssume now \\(k\\ge1\\).  \nWe shall replace \\(k\\) of the vertical lines of \\(\\mathcal N\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (existence of a new sunny line)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal S\\) be a finite collection of lines already constructed, and let \\(P\\in I_n\\) be a point that does **not** lie on any sunny line of \\(\\mathcal S\\).  \nThen there exists a sunny line through \\(P\\) whose slope is different from the slopes of all lines in \\(\\mathcal S\\).\n\n*Proof.* The set of slopes of the lines in \\(\\mathcal S\\) is finite; denote it by \\(M\\subset\\mathbb R\\cup\\{\\infty\\}\\).  \nChoose a real number \\(\\alpha\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\cup M .\n\\]\n\nSuch an \\(\\alpha\\) exists because we are removing only finitely many numbers from the uncountable set \\(\\mathbb R\\cup\\{\\infty\\}\\).  \nThe line through \\(P\\) with slope \\(\\alpha\\) is sunny and cannot coincide with any line of \\(\\mathcal S\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction of the sunny lines\n\nEnumerate the vertical lines of \\(\\mathcal N\\) as \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-3}\\) where \\(V_i\\) is the line \\(x=i\\).  \nWe replace the first \\(k\\) of them, say \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_k\\), by sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) constructed inductively:\n\n*Step \\(i\\) ( \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) ).*  \nLet \\(\\mathcal S_{i-1}= \\{L_h,L_v,L_d\\}\\cup\\bigl(\\mathcal N\\setminus\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{i-1}\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_{i-1}\\}\\).  \nPick any point \\(P_i\\) of the vertical line \\(V_i\\) (for instance \\((i,1)\\)); this point is not on any sunny line of \\(\\mathcal S_{i-1}\\) because we have not created any sunny line through it yet.  \nApply Lemma 3 to obtain a sunny line \\(S_i\\) through \\(P_i\\) whose slope is new.  \n\nThus after the \\(k\\)-th step we have the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\n\\{L_h,L_v,L_d\\}\\cup\n\\bigl(\\mathcal N\\setminus\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_k\\}\\bigr)\\cup\n\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(S_i\\)’s) and \\(3+(n-1)-k = n+2\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Reducing the total to \\(n\\) lines\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) has \\(n+2\\) lines, i.e. two more than required.  \nWe delete any two non‑sunny lines that are **not** needed for the covering.  \nA convenient choice is to delete the two horizontal lines \\(y=1\\) and \\(y=2\\) (if they are present); the points that were covered only by those horizontals are now covered by the sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) because each deleted horizontal line intersected the vertical line \\(x=i\\) at the point \\((i,1)\\) or \\((i,2)\\), and that very point lies on the sunny line \\(S_i\\) constructed through it.  \n\nConsequently the remaining set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal G_k=\n\\{L_h,L_v,L_d\\}\\cup\n\\bigl(\\mathcal N\\setminus\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_k,\\,y=1,\\,y=2\\}\\bigr)\\cup\n\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Verification of the covering property\n\n*Outer diagonal* – by Lemma 1 every point with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lies on \\(L_h\\), \\(L_v\\) or \\(L_d\\), all of which belong to \\(\\mathcal G_k\\).\n\n*Interior points* – let \\((a,b)\\in I_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-3\\) and \\(a\\notin\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (still present in \\(\\mathcal G_k\\)) contains the point.\n\n* If \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) then the point lies on the sunny line \\(S_a\\) (by construction \\(S_a\\) passes through \\((a,1)\\) and therefore also through \\((a,b)\\) because the slope of \\(S_a\\) is not vertical).\n\n* If \\(a=n-2\\) then \\(b\\le2\\).  \n   – If \\(b=1\\) or \\(b=2\\) the point was originally on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\); after the deletion of that horizontal line the point is still on the sunny line \\(S_{n-2}\\) (which was drawn through \\((n-2,1)\\) and has non‑vertical slope, hence also passes through \\((n-2,2)\\)).  \n   – If \\(b\\le0\\) the point does not belong to \\(I_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b=1\\); this point is on the sunny line \\(S_{n-1}\\) constructed through \\((n-1,1)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal G_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7. Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a set \\(\\mathcal G_k\\) of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one line of the set, and  \n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nTogether with the obvious bound (1), this proves that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
