---------------------------\n### Consequences\n\nThe lemma forces the presence of the diagonals \\(\\ell_s\\) for all \\(s=3,4,\\dots ,n+1\\).  \nThere are exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n+1)-3+1 = n-1\n\\]\n\nsuch lines, all of which are non‑sunny. Hence among the \\(n\\) required lines at least \\(n-1\\) are non‑sunny, and therefore\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-(n-1)=1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe case \\(s=2\\) does **not** give a forced line, because \\(D_2=\\{(1,1)\\}\\) consists of a single point that can be covered by any line through \\((1,1)\\), sunny or not.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Constructions\n\n#### 1.  Configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n* the \\(n-1\\) forced diagonals \\(\\ell_s\\) for \\(s=3,4,\\dots ,n+1\\);\n* the vertical line \\(v:\\;x=1\\).\n\nAll these lines have slope \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\); none is sunny, so \\(k=0\\).\n\nNow let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Its sum \\(s=a+b\\) lies in \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).\n\n* If \\(s\\ge 3\\) then \\((a,b)\\in D_s\\) and lies on the diagonal \\(\\ell_s\\).\n* If \\(s=2\\) then \\((a,b)=(1,1)\\), which lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\n#### 2.  Configuration with \\(k=1\\)\n\nKeep the same \\(n-1\\) diagonals \\(\\ell_s\\) (\\(s=3,\\dots ,n+1\\)) and replace the vertical line by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\ns:\\; y = 2x-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(s\\) is sunny, giving exactly one sunny line.  \nThe covering argument is identical to the previous case, because the diagonals already cover all points of \\(T_n\\). Hence the required condition holds with \\(k=1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition contains at least two sunny lines. By the Lemma, the family must contain the \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny diagonals \\(\\ell_s\\) for \\(s=3,\\dots ,n+1\\). Since the total number of lines is \\(n\\), at most one line remains free to be chosen arbitrarily. Hence at most one line can be sunny, contradicting the assumption \\(k\\ge 2\\). Therefore no configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Result\n\nThe only non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
