            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\},\n\\qquad \n\\ell_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_s\\) has slope \\(-1\\); consequently it is **not** sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nIf a line \\(\\mathcal L\\) is not equal to a diagonal \\(\\ell_s\\), then \\(\\mathcal L\\) meets each diagonal \\(\\ell_s\\) in at most one point; equivalently \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at most one element of each set \\(D_s\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo distinct points of a diagonal have slope \\(-1\\). If a line \\(\\mathcal L\\neq\\ell_s\\) contained two such points, those two points would determine the unique line of slope \\(-1\\) passing through them, namely \\(\\ell_s\\), contradicting \\(\\mathcal L\\neq\\ell_s\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Forced diagonals\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\{\\ell_s\\mid 3\\le s\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\n(the diagonals whose point‑sets have size at least two).  \nSuppose, for contradiction, that some diagonal \\(\\ell_t\\in\\mathcal D\\) is **absent** from \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\nFor each \\(s\\) the set \\(D_s\\) has cardinality \\(|D_s|=s-1\\).  \nIf \\(\\ell_s\\notin\\mathcal F\\) then, by Lemma 1, each line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) can cover at most one point of \\(D_s\\); therefore covering all points of \\(D_s\\) requires at least \\(s-1\\) distinct lines that are not \\(\\ell_s\\).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{s\\mid 3\\le s\\le n+1,\\;\\ell_s\\notin\\mathcal F\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of omitted diagonals.  \nFor the largest element \\(s_{\\max}=\\max S\\) we need at least \\(s_{\\max}-1\\) distinct non‑diagonal lines.  \nEach of the remaining \\(|S|-1\\) omitted diagonals still lacks at least one point after those \\(s_{\\max}-1\\) lines have been used (because a single line can supply at most one point to each diagonal). Hence the total number of lines that are **not** diagonals must be at least  \n\n\\[\n(s_{\\max}-1)+(|S|-1)=\\max_{s\\in S}(s-1)+|S|-1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand, the family \\(\\mathcal F\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, of which \\(|\\mathcal D|-|S|=(n-1)-|S|\\) are the diagonals that are present. Consequently the number of lines that are not diagonals is at most  \n\n\\[\nn-[(n-1)-|S|]=|S|+1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) and (6) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\max_{s\\in S}(s-1)+|S|-1\\le |S|+1,\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to  \n\n\\[\n\\max_{s\\in S}(s-1)\\le 2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSince every omitted diagonal satisfies \\(s\\ge 3\\), we have \\(s-1\\ge 2\\).  \nThus (7) forces \\(\\max_{s\\in S}(s-1)=2\\), i.e. the only possible omitted diagonal would be \\(\\ell_{3}\\) (for which \\(s-1=2\\)) and we would have \\(|S|=1\\).\n\nAssume \\(\\ell_{3}\\) is omitted. Then \\(|S|=1\\) and (6) tells us that at most two lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) are not diagonals.  \nBut the set \\(D_{3}\\) consists of the two points \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\); covering both without \\(\\ell_{3}\\) indeed requires **exactly** two distinct non‑diagonal lines, leaving no line left to cover the single point \\((1,1)\\) of \\(D_{2}\\) (which is not on any diagonal \\(\\ell_s\\) with \\(s\\ge 3\\)).  \nHence \\(\\ell_{3}\\) cannot be omitted either.\n\nTherefore every diagonal \\(\\ell_s\\) with \\(3\\le s\\le n+1\\) must belong to \\(\\mathcal F\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal F\\text{ contains the }n-1\\text{ lines }\\ell_{3},\\ell_{4},\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}} .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for sunny lines\n\nThe family has exactly \\(n\\) lines, and (8) already supplies \\(n-1\\) of them, all of which are non‑sunny.  \nHence at most one of the remaining lines can be sunny:\n\n\\[\nk\\le 1 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence of configurations\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n-1\\) forced diagonals (8) and add the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \nThe vertical line is not sunny, so the whole family contains no sunny line.  \n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) let \\(s=a+b\\).  \nIf \\(s\\ge 3\\) then \\((a,b)\\in D_s\\subset\\ell_s\\); if \\(s=2\\) then \\((a,b)=(1,1)\\) lies on \\(x=1\\).  \nThus every required point is covered.\n\n*Case \\(k=1\\).*  \nReplace the vertical line by a sunny line, for example  \n\n\\[\ny=2x-1 .\n\\]\n\nIts slope \\(2\\) differs from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore exactly one line of the family is sunny.  \nThe covering argument is unchanged: points with \\(s\\ge 3\\) lie on the corresponding diagonal, and the point \\((1,1)\\) (the only point with \\(s=2\\)) lies on \\(y=2x-1\\).  \n\nHence configurations exist for both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nInequality (9) shows that no configuration can have \\(k\\ge 2\\); the explicit constructions exhibit the two admissible values. Therefore the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
