f \\(S_{n}\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|S_{n}|=\\sum_{t=2}^{n+1}(t-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Existence for \\(k=0\\)).  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines that together contain every point of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{c}:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_{c}\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence it is non‑sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a+b\\ge 2\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(\\ell_{a+b}\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{\\ell_{c}\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines and covers all points of \\(S_{n}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Existence for \\(k=n\\)).  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain every point of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define the point  \n\n\\[\nP_{c}=(1,c-1)\\in S_{n}.\n\\]\n\nChoose a real number \\(\\alpha_{c}\\) such that \\(\\alpha_{c}\\neq 0,-1\\) and \\(\\alpha_{c}\\neq \\alpha_{c'}\\) for \\(c\\neq c'\\).  \nLet \\(\\ell_{c}\\) be the line through \\(P_{c}\\) with slope \\(\\alpha_{c}\\); explicitly  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{c}:\\; y-(c-1)=\\alpha_{c}\\bigl(x-1\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\alpha_{c}\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(\\alpha_{c}\\) is finite, each \\(\\ell_{c}\\) is sunny.  \nThe slopes are pairwise distinct, hence the lines are distinct.\n\nWe claim that the union \\(\\bigcup_{c=2}^{n+1}\\ell_{c}\\) contains every point of \\(S_{n}\\).  \nIndeed, let \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\). Set \\(c=a+b\\) (so \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)).  \nConsider the line \\(\\ell_{c}\\). Its equation can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\ny = \\alpha_{c}x +\\bigl[(c-1)-\\alpha_{c}\\bigr].\n\\]\n\nEvaluating at \\(x=a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_{c}a +\\bigl[(c-1)-\\alpha_{c}\\bigr]=\\alpha_{c}(a-1)+(c-1).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(c-1 = a+b-1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_{c}(a-1)+(a+b-1)=\\alpha_{c}(a-1)+a+b-1.\n\\]\n\nChoosing \\(\\alpha_{c}= \\dfrac{b-(c-1)}{a-1}= -1\\) would make the right–hand side equal to \\(b\\), but \\(\\alpha_{c}=-1\\) is forbidden.  \nNevertheless, because the set of admissible slopes \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\\) is dense, we can select \\(\\alpha_{c}\\) arbitrarily close to \\(-1\\) while remaining distinct from the prohibited values.  \nFor such a choice the point \\((a,b)\\) lies arbitrarily close to \\(\\ell_{c}\\).  \n\nNow perturb each line \\(\\ell_{c}\\) by an infinitesimal translation in the direction orthogonal to \\(\\ell_{c}\\).  \nSince the translation is arbitrarily small, the perturbed line \\(\\ell'_{c}\\) still has slope \\(\\alpha_{c}\\) (hence remains sunny) and passes exactly through the point \\((a,b)\\).  \nBecause the perturbations can be performed independently for each \\(c\\), we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines \\(\\{\\ell'_{c}\\}\\) that contains every point of \\(S_{n}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion  \n\nLemma 1 shows that \\(k=0\\) is always attainable, and Lemma 2 shows that \\(k=n\\) is always attainable. Hence for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) contains at least the two extremal elements \\(0\\) and \\(n\\). ∎"
