cause \\(k\\le n\\)), the point \\((a,a+1)\\) belongs to the required set and is already covered by a sunny line.\n\n   *Step\u202f3 \u2013 remaining points.*  \n   All points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge k+1\\) are not yet covered.  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(k+1\\le c\\le n\\) introduce the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   V_c:\\; x = c .\n   \\]\n\n   There are exactly \\(n-k\\) such vertical lines, and together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines we have \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines.\n\n   Every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) falls into one of the two groups above, therefore it lies on at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The constructions give a feasible configuration for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   Since \\(k\\) cannot exceed the total number of lines, the set of all possible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe must decide, for a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\), which non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) admit a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* (C1) every point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n* (C2) exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are *sunny*, i.e. are not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions: the \\(x\\)-axis (\\(y= \\text{const}\\)), the \\(y\\)-axis (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)), and the line \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Upper bound  \n\nSince \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, at most \\(n\\) of them can be sunny. Hence  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The case \\(k=0\\)  \n\nConsider the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_{c}: x=c\\qquad (c=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nAll of them are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore none is sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(V_{a}\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{V_{1},\\dots ,V_{n}\\}\\) satisfies (C1) and (C2) with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).  \n\n##### 3.1 Sunny lines  \n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\; y = m_{i}x + (i+1),\\qquad \nm_{i}= \\frac{i}{i+1}.\n\\]\n\nThe slopes \\(m_{i}\\) are distinct and satisfy  \n\n\\[\nm_{i}\\neq 0,\\qquad m_{i}\\neq \\infty,\\qquad m_{i}\\neq -1,\n\\]\n\nhence every \\(L_{i}\\) is sunny. The lines are distinct because they have different slopes.\n\n##### 3.2 Points covered by the sunny lines  \n\nFor each \\(i\\) we compute the point of \\(L_{i}\\) with abscissa \\(x=i\\):\n\n\\[\ny = m_{i}i + (i+1)=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,i + (i+1)=i+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the lattice point  \n\n\\[\nP_{i}=(i,i+1)\n\\]\n\nlies on \\(L_{i}\\). Since  \n\n\\[\ni+(i+1)=2i+1\\le 2k+1\\le n+1,\n\\]\n\n\\(P_{i}\\in T_{n}\\). Consequently each \\(L_{i}\\) already covers at least one required point.\n\n##### 3.3 Remaining points and vertical lines  \n\nAll points of \\(T_{n}\\) whose first coordinate satisfies \\(a\\ge k+1\\) are not yet guaranteed to be covered.  \nFor every integer \\(c\\) with  \n\n\\[\nk+1\\le c\\le n\n\\]\n\nintroduce the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_{c}: x=c .\n\\]\n\nThere are exactly \\(n-k\\) such lines.  \n\nNow take the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L = \\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}\\cup\\{V_{c}\\mid k+1\\le c\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIt contains \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the first \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n##### 3.4 Verification of condition (C1)\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le k\\):*  \nIf \\((a,b)=(a,a+1)\\) then it lies on the sunny line \\(L_{a}\\) as shown above.  \nIf \\(b\\neq a+1\\) then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(k+1)=n-k\\).  \