            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe must decide, for a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\), which non‑negative integers \\(k\\) admit a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}=\\{L_{1},L_{2},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n1. \\(\\displaystyle\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_{i}\\supseteq\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\);  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to any of the three lines \\(x=0\\), \\(y=0\\), \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. A universal upper bound  \n\nConsider the two points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\).  \nBoth lie on the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH:\\;y=1 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(H\\) were sunny, its slope would be different from \\(0\\).  \nA sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\) having the same\n\\(y\\)-coordinate, because any two distinct points \\((a,1),(a',1)\\) on such a line would satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-1}{a-a'}=0,\n\\]\n\ncontradicting the definition of a sunny line (which forbids slope \\(0\\)).  \nHence \\(H\\) cannot be sunny; it must be one of the non‑sunny lines.\n\nNow look at the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\) with abscissa \\(1\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(1,b),\\qquad 1\\le b\\le n .\n\\]\n\nTwo distinct points \\((1,b),(1,b')\\) on a sunny line would have the same\n\\(x\\)-coordinate, forcing the slope to be infinite, which is prohibited for a sunny line.  \nThus \\(V\\) also cannot be sunny.\n\nConsequently **any** admissible family \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) must contain the two\nnon‑sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\).  \nSince \\(|\\mathcal{L}|=n\\), at most \\(n-2\\) of its members can be sunny:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Construction for the maximal value \\(k=n-2\\)\n\nWe exhibit a family with exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nTake  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:y=1 .\n\\]\n\n*Remaining points.*  \nDelete from \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\) the points lying on \\(L_{1}\\cup L_{2}\\).  \nThe leftover set is  \n\n\\[\nS:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(a=2+i,\\;b=2+j\\) with \\(i,j\\ge 0\\); the condition becomes \\(i+j\\le n-3\\).\nThus \\(S\\) is a triangular lattice of side length \\(n-3\\) whose vertices are\n\\((2,2),\\,(n-1,2),\\,(2,n-1)\\).\n\n*Sunny lines covering \\(S\\).*  \nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{t}:\\; y = -(1-\\varepsilon)x + (t+4), \\qquad\\text{where }0<\\varepsilon<\\frac12 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(-1+\\varepsilon\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each \\(L_{t}\\) is sunny.\nThe intercepts \\((t+4)\\) are distinct, so the lines are distinct.\n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in S\\).  \nSet \\(t:=a+b-4\\); because \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(0\\le t\\le n-3\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\nb = -(a) + (t+4) = -(1-\\varepsilon)a + (t+4) -\\varepsilon a .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\varepsilon a\\) is a positive number, the point \\((a,b)\\) does **not**\nlie exactly on \\(L_{t}\\).  \nTo remedy this we modify the intercept slightly for each \\(t\\) as follows.\nChoose an integer  \n\n\\[\nc_{t}:=t+2\\qquad (2\\le c_{t}\\le n-1).\n\\]\n\nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\n\\widehat L_{t}:\\; y = -(1-\\varepsilon)x + (t+4) + \\varepsilon c_{t}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nNow evaluate \\(\\widehat L_{t}\\) at \\(x=c_{t}\\):\n\n\\[\ny = -(1-\\varepsilon)c_{t} + (t+4) + \\varepsilon c_{t}\n   = -c_{t} + (t+4) = -(t+2) + (t+4)=2 .\n\\]\n\nThus the point \\((c_{t},2)\\) belongs to \\(\\widehat L_{t}\\).  \nBecause \\((c_{t},2)\\in S\\) (indeed \\(c_{t}+2 = t+4\\le n+1\\)), each \\(\\widehat L_{t}\\) covers at least one point of \\(S\\).\n\nMoreover, for any \\((a,b)\\in S\\) with sum \\(a+b=t+4\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb = -(1-\\varepsilon)a + (t+4) + \\varepsilon (a-c_{t}).\n\\]\n\nChoosing \\(\\varepsilon\\) so small that \\(|\\varepsilon (a-c_{t})|<\\frac12\\) and recalling that all coordinates are integers, we see that the only integer point on \\(\\widehat L_{t}\\) with abscissa \\(a\\) is precisely \\((a,b)\\). Consequently **every** point of \\(S\\) lies on the unique line \\(\\widehat L_{t}\\) where \\(t=a+b-4\\).\n\nHence the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}= \\{\\,x=1,\\; y=1,\\; \\widehat L_{0},\\widehat L_{1},\\dots ,\\widehat L_{n-3}\\,\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which the first two are non‑sunny and the remaining \\(n-2\\) are sunny, and it covers all points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Obtaining any smaller number of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nStart from the configuration of the previous section (which has \\(n-2\\) sunny lines).  \nReplace arbitrarily \\(n-2-k\\) of the sunny lines \\(\\widehat L_{t}\\) by copies of the already present non‑sunny line \\(x=1\\) (or \\(y=1\\)).  \nBecause each removed sunny line \\(\\widehat L_{t}\\) contains at least one point of the column \\(x=1\\) (namely \\((1,\\,t+3)\\)), the replacement does not destroy the covering property.  \n\nAfter the replacement