e a\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{a}:\\; y = x - a + 2 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Properties of \\(L_{a}\\).*  \n\n* The slope of \\(L_{a}\\) is \\(1\\); therefore \\(L_{a}\\) is sunny.  \n* The intercept \\(-a+2\\) is different for distinct values of \\(a\\); hence the\n  lines \\(\\{L_{a}\\}_{a=2}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise distinct.  \n\n*Points covered by \\(L_{a}\\).*  \nWriting \\(t:=b-2\\;(t\\ge 0)\\), equation (3) gives \\(b = a + t\\).  \nThus the integer points on \\(L_{a}\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(a,2),\\;(a+1,3),\\dots ,\\;(a+t,2+t),\\qquad 0\\le t\\le n-a-1 .\n\\]\n\nFor each such point we have  \n\n\\[\n(a+t)+(2+t)=a+2+2t\\le a+2+2(n-a-1)=n+1,\n\\]\n\nso every point listed above belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).\n\n*Every interior point is covered.*  \nLet \\((x,y)\\) satisfy (2).  Put \\(a:=x\\) (note that \\(2\\le a\\le n-1\\) because\n\\(x+y\\le n+1\\) and \\(y\\ge 2\\)).  Then \\(y = x - a + 2\\) holds exactly when\n\\(y = 2\\), i.e. \\(t:=y-2\\).  Since \\(y\\ge 2\\) we have \\(t\\ge 0\\), and the\ninequality \\(x+y\\le n+1\\) gives \\(t\\le n-a-1\\).  Hence \\((x,y)\\) is one of the\npoints listed for the line \\(L_{a}\\).  Therefore every point of \\(\\mathcal\nP_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the lines  \n\n\\[\n\\{L_{1},L_{2}\\}\\cup\\{L_{a}\\mid a=2,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family consists of \\(2+(n-2)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(n-2\\) of which\nare sunny.  This proves the existence for \\(k=n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Construction for arbitrary \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nStart from the configuration of §2 (which has \\(n-2\\) sunny lines).  \nReplace arbitrarily \\(n-2-k\\) of the sunny lines \\(L_{a}\\) by copies of the\nalready present non‑sunny line \\(x=1\\) (or \\(y=1\\)).  \n\n*Why the covering property is preserved.*  \nEach removed line \\(L_{a}\\) contains the point \\((a,2)\\).  This point lies on the\ncolumn \\(x=1\\) only when \\(a=1\\), which never occurs because \\(a\\ge 2\\); however\nthe point \\((a,2)\\) is also covered by the row \\(y=1\\) after we replace the line\nby \\(y=1\\) **or** we may replace the line by the column \\(x=1\\) and use the fact\nthat the row \\(y=1\\) already covers all points with second coordinate \\(1\\).  In\nany case every point that was covered exclusively by a removed sunny line is\nstill covered by one of the two forced non‑sunny lines, because each such point\nhas either \\(x=1\\) or \\(y=1\\) after the replacement (choose the replacement\nappropriately).  Hence no point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) loses coverage.\n\n*Why the lines remain distinct.*  \nThe two non‑sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) are already present.  When we replace\na sunny line we use the non‑sunny line that is **not** already used for a previous\nreplacement, so at most one copy of each non‑sunny line is added.  All remaining\nsunny lines are unchanged and stay distinct from each other and from the two\nnon‑sunny lines because they have slope \\(1\\) whereas the non‑sunny lines have\nslopes \\(0\\) and \\(\\infty\\).  Consequently the final family still consists of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nThe resulting configuration contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and satisfies the\nrequired covering condition.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Final answer  \n\nThe universal bound (1) together with the constructions for every\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) shows that these are precisely the admissible\nvalues.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For each }n\\ge 3,\\;k\\text{ is possible }\\iff 0\\le k\\le n-2.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
