-------------------\n### Lemma 1 (existence of a configuration with no sunny line)\n\nDefine the following three families of non‑sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\mid c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the three lines\n\\(x=a\\in\\mathcal V\\), \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H\\) and \\(x+y=a+b\\in\\mathcal D\\).\n\nChoose the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n\\[\nL_i=\n\\begin{cases}\nx=i & 1\\le i\\le \\lfloor n/2\\rfloor,\\\\[2pt]\ny=i-\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor & \\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1\\le i\\le n-1,\\\\[2pt]\nx+y=n+1 & i=n .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in (1) belong to \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\); hence none is sunny.\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have:\n\n* if \\(a\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\);\n* if \\(b\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\);\n* otherwise \\(a+b\\ge n+1\\), and because \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we must have \\(a+b=n+1\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines (1).  \nConsequently a configuration with \\(n\\) distinct lines and **zero** sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (no configuration with exactly one sunny line)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration \\(\\mathcal C\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly one sunny line \\(L\\).  \nLet \\(\\mathcal C'=\\mathcal C\\setminus\\{L\\}\\); then \\(|\\mathcal C'|=n-1\\) and every line of \\(\\mathcal C'\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nPartition \\(\\mathcal C'\\) into three subfamilies:\n\\[\n\\mathcal V'=\\{\\,\\text{vertical lines in }\\mathcal C'\\,\\},\\;\n\\mathcal H'=\\{\\,\\text{horizontal lines in }\\mathcal C'\\,\\},\\;\n\\mathcal D'=\\{\\,\\text{diagonal lines of slope }-1\\text{ in }\\mathcal C'\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|\\mathcal C'|=n-1\\), at least one of the three families contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{3}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) lines.  \nWithout loss of generality suppose \\(|\\mathcal V'|\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{3}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\nThe vertical lines of \\(\\mathcal V'\\) can cover at most \\(|\\mathcal V'|\\) distinct abscissae.  \nConsequently there are at least  \n\n\\[\nn-\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n-1}{3}\\bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\bigl\\lceil\\tfrac{2n+1}{3}\\bigr\\rceil\n\\]\n\ndifferent integers \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) for which the line \\(x=a\\) does **not** belong to \\(\\mathcal V'\\).\n\nPick any such integer \\(a\\).  \nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nR_a=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid b\\ge 1\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(R_a\\) have the same abscissa, so they cannot be covered by a sunny line (otherwise that line would be vertical).  \nHence each point of \\(R_a\\) must be covered by a line of \\(\\mathcal H'\\) or \\(\\mathcal D'\\).  \nBut a horizontal line can contain at most one point of \\(R_a\\) (different \\(b\\)’s give different ordinates), and a diagonal line of slope \\(-1\\) can contain at most one point of \\(R_a\\) as well (different \\(b\\)’s give different sums \\(a+b\\)).  \n\nTherefore \\(|\\mathcal H'|+|\\mathcal D'|\\) must be at least \\(|R_a|\\).  \nSince \\(|R_a|=n+1-a\\ge 2\\) (because \\(a\\le n-1\\)), we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal H'|+|\\mathcal D'|\\ge 2.\n\\]\n\nAdding the bound on \\(|\\mathcal V'|\\) we get  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal C'|=|\\mathcal V'|+|\\mathcal H'|+|\\mathcal D'|\\ge\n\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n-1}{3}\\bigr\\rfloor+2.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\) this inequality forces \\(|\\mathcal C'|\\ge n\\), contradicting \\(|\\mathcal C'|=n-1\\).  \nHence a configuration with exactly one sunny line cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (existence of configurations with any prescribed \\(k\\ge 2\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe start from the zero‑sunny configuration of Lemma 1 and modify it.\n\n1. **Selection of \\(k\\) points on the hypotenuse.**  \n   Choose any \\(k\\) distinct points of \\(T_n\\); denote them  \n   \\[\n   P_j=(a_j,b_j)\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,k),\n   \\]\n   where \\(a_j+b_j=n+1\\) and the pairs \\((a_j,b_j)\\) are all different.\n\n2. **Construction of sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(j\\) pick a slope \\(m_j\\) such that  \n   \\[\n   m_j\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\\qquad m_i\\neq m_j\\;(i\\neq j).\n   \\]\n   Define the sunny line  \n   \\[\n   S_j:\\; y=m_j x + (b_j-m_j a_j).\n   \\]\n   By construction \\(S_j\\) passes through \\(P_j\\) and is sunny.\n\n3. **Removal of the corresponding non‑sunny lines.**  \n   In the zero‑sunny configuration (1) the point \\(P_j\\) is covered either by a vertical line \\(x=a_j\\), a horizontal line \\(y=b_j\\), or the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n   Delete from (1) **one** of those non‑sunny lines for each \\(j\\); because the points \\(P_j\\) are distinct, the deleted lines are distinct.\n\n   After the deletions we have removed exactly \\(k\\) non‑sunny lines and added the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).  \n   The total number of lines is still \\(n\\).\n\n4. **Verification of coverage.**  \n   *Points of \\(T_n\\).*  \n   Each point \\(P_j\\) now lies on the sunny line \\(S_j\\).  \n   The remaining points of \\(T_n\\) (if any) are still covered by the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\), which was not deleted because at most one of the deleted lines could be that diagonal (we simply avoid deleting it when \\(k<n\\); if \\(k=n\\) we delete it and replace it by a sunny line, which still covers its former points).\n\n   *Points of \\(S_n\\setminus T_n\\).*  \n   Such a point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n\\).  \n   In the original zero‑sunny configuration it was covered by a vertical line \\(x=a\\) or a hor