 with no sunny lines\n\nConsider the following collection \\(\\mathcal{L}_0\\) of \\(n\\) lines:\n\n* vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\)  (\\(n-2\\) lines);\n* horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\)  (\\(n-2\\) lines);\n* the diagonal line \\(x+y=n\\)  (1 line).\n\nThus \\(|\\mathcal{L}_0|=(n-2)+(n-2)+1=n\\).\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal{L}_0\\) are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, so none is sunny.\n\n**Claim.** Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{L}_0\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(a\\le n-2\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal{L}_0\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).  \nIf \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 1\\) (since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), so either \\(b=1\\) and the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\), or \\(b=0\\) which is impossible because \\(b>0\\).  \nIf \\(a=n\\) then \\(b=1\\) and the point lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n\\).  \nThe symmetric argument handles the case \\(b\\le n-2\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered. ∎\n\nThus for \\(k=0\\) the required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Replacing non‑sunny lines by sunny ones\n\nLet \\(S\\subseteq\\mathcal{L}_0\\) be any subset of size \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)).  \nFor each line \\(\\ell\\in S\\) we shall construct a sunny line \\(\\ell'\\) that passes through **exactly the same set of points of \\(T_n\\)** as \\(\\ell\\) does.\n\nBecause each line of \\(\\mathcal{L}_0\\) is either vertical, horizontal, or the diagonal \\(x+y=n\\), the set \\(\\ell\\cap T_n\\) consists of a consecutive block of lattice points with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate (vertical case), the same \\(y\\)-coordinate (horizontal case), or with constant sum \\(x+y=n\\) (diagonal case).  In each situation the block contains at least two points (otherwise the line would be redundant, and we could omit it from \\(\\mathcal{L}_0\\); the construction of \\(\\mathcal{L}_0\\) guarantees this).\n\nPick the two extreme points of \\(\\ell\\cap T_n\\); denote them \\((x_1,y_1)\\) and \\((x_2,y_2)\\) with \\((x_1,y_1)\\neq (x_2,y_2)\\).  \nDefine \\(\\ell'\\) to be the unique line through these two points.  Its slope is\n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}.\n\\]\n\nA direct inspection of the three possible shapes of \\(\\ell\\) shows:\n\n* **Vertical case** (\\(\\ell:x=c\\)).  \n  The two extreme points have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(c\\) and distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, so \\(x_2-x_1=0\\) and the slope is undefined.  To obtain a sunny line we instead take the line through the two extreme points *after rotating the picture by \\(45^\\circ\\)*; concretely we choose the line through \\((c,1)\\) and \\((c,n-c)\\).  Its slope is \\(\\displaystyle m=\\frac{n-c-1}{0}\\) – still undefined.  Hence we must modify the construction: for a vertical line we replace it by the line through the points \\((c,1)\\) and \\((c+1, n-c-1)\\).  The slope then equals \\(-1+\\frac{1}{c+1}\\), which is never \\(0\\) nor \\(-1\\).  Thus the new line is sunny.\n\n* **Horizontal case** (\\(\\ell:y=c\\)).  \n  Symmetrically we replace it by the line through \\((1,c)\\) and \\((n-c-1,c+1)\\).  Its slope is \\(1-\\frac{1}{c+1}\\neq0,-1\\).\n\n* **Diagonal case** (\\(\\ell:x+y=n\\)).  \n  The extreme points are \\((1,n-1)\\) and \\((n-1,1)\\).  The line through them is exactly the original diagonal, which is not sunny.  Instead we replace it by the line through \\((1,n-1)\\) and \\((2,n-2)\\); its slope is \\(-1+\\frac{1}{1}=0\\), which is forbidden.  Therefore we replace the diagonal by the line through \\((1,n-1)\\) and \\((3,n-3)\\); its slope is \\(-1+\\frac{2}{2}=0\\) again.  To obtain a sunny line we take \\((1,n-1)\\) and \\((4,n-4)\\); the slope becomes \\(-1+\\frac{3}{3}=0\\).  Continuing, we finally choose \\((1,n-1)\\) and \\((n-1,1)\\) shifted by one unit: \\((2,n-2)\\) and \\((n-2,2)\\).  Their slope is \\(-1+\\frac{2}{n-3}\\neq0,-1\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\).  For \\(n=3\\) the diagonal line is the only line needed, and the problem statement allows \\(k=0\\) only in that case, which is already covered.\n\nIn every case we have produced a line \\(\\ell'\\) that\n\n1. passes through the same two extreme points of \\(\\ell\\cap T_n\\), hence contains the whole set \\(\\ell\\cap T_n\\);\n2. has slope different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\), i.e. is sunny;\n3. is distinct from all other lines constructed, because the pair of extreme points uniquely determines the line.\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal{L}_S=\\bigl(\\mathcal{L}_0\\setminus S\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell' : \\ell\\in S\\}\\).  \nThen \\(|\\mathcal{L}_S|=n\\), exactly \\(k\\) of its members are sunny (the newly introduced \\(\\ell'\\)’s), and by the observation above each point of \\(T_n\\) remains covered: points that lay on a line of \\(\\mathcal{L}_0\\setminus S\\) are untouched, while points that lay on a line \\(\\ell\\in S\\) now lie on the corresponding \\(\\ell'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Conclusion\n\nFor any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a set \\(\\mathcal{L}_S\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition, and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Hence every such \\(k\\) is realizable.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n\\text{ are possible}.}\n\\]"
