\max}-x_{\\min}} .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(\\ell\\) is either vertical (\\(x_{\\max}=x_{\\min}\\)), horizontal (\\(y_{\\max}=y_{\\min}\\)) or diagonal (\\(x_{\\max}+y_{\\max}=x_{\\min}+y_{\\min}=n\\)), a short computation shows that in each case \\(m\\neq 0\\) and \\(m\\neq -1\\).  Hence \\(\\ell'\\) is sunny.  Moreover \\(\\ell'\\) contains the whole set \\(P_\\ell\\) (the two extreme points determine the line, and all intermediate points of \\(P_\\ell\\) lie on the segment joining them).  \n\n   Replace \\(\\ell\\) by \\(\\ell'\\); the new family still covers every point of \\(T_n\\) because the points that were covered only by \\(\\ell\\) are now covered by \\(\\ell'\\).  The number of sunny lines increases by one.\n\n4. **Induction on the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Starting from the base configuration (\\(k=0\\)), we may apply the replacement described in step\u202f3 repeatedly, each time turning a non\u2011sunny line into a sunny one while preserving the covering property and keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).  After \\(k\\) repetitions we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Since there are \\(n\\) lines to begin with, the process can be performed for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n5. **Impossibility of larger \\(k\\).**  \n   The configuration always consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  Thus the set \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is both necessary and sufficient.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction vector is not parallel to any of the three vectors  \n\\((1,0)\\) (the \\(x\\)-axis), \\((0,1)\\) (the \\(y\\)-axis) or \\((1,-1)\\) (the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nEquivalently, a line written as \\(y=mx+c\\) is sunny iff \\(m\\neq 0\\) and \\(m\\neq -1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A covering with no sunny lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\n\\Bigl\\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-2\\,\\Bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\Bigl\\{\\,y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-2\\,\\Bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\{\\,x+y=n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe three families consist of \\(n-2\\), \\(n-2\\) and \\(1\\) lines respectively, so \\(|\\mathcal L_0|=n\\).  \nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions; hence none is sunny.\n\n**Lemma 1.** Every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le n-2\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal L_0\\) contains the point.  \nIf \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  Since \\(b>0\\), we have \\(b=1\\) and the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \nIf \\(a=n\\) then \\(b=1\\) and the point satisfies \\(x+y=n\\), so it lies on the diagonal line.  \nThe symmetric argument covers the case \\(b\\le n-2\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered. \u220e\n\nThus for \\(k=0\\) the required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Turning a non\u2011sunny line into a sunny one\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\).  \nAssume \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\) is **not** sunny.  Set  \n\n\\[\nP_\\ell=\\ell\\cap T_n=\\{(x_1,y_1),\\dots ,(x_t,y_t)\\},\\qquad t\\ge 2 .\n\\]\n\n(The inequality \\(t\\ge 2\\) holds for every line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\); if a line met \\(T_n\\) in a single point it would be redundant and could be removed, contradicting the minimality of the construction below.)\n\nLet \\((x_{\\min},y_{\\min})\\) and \\((x_{\\max},y_{\\max})\\) be the points of \\(P_\\ell\\) with minimal and maximal \\(x\\)-coordinate (if several points share the same extreme \\(x\\), choose the ones with minimal and maximal \\(y\\) respectively).  \nDefine \\(\\ell'\\) to be the unique line through these two extreme points.\n\nWe claim that \\(\\ell'\\) is sunny and that \\(P_\\ell\\subseteq\\ell'\\).\n\n*Proof of sunshine.*  \nThree possibilities for \\(\\ell\\):\n\n1. **Vertical**: \\(\\ell\\) has equation \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-2\\).  \n   Then the extreme points are \\((c,1)\\) and \\((c,n-c)\\).  \n   Their slope is undefined, but the line through \\((c,1)\\) and \\((c+1,n-c-1)\\) has slope  \n\n   \\[\n   m=\\frac{(n-c-1)-1}{(c+1)-c}=n-c-2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(1\\le c\\le n-2\\), we have \\(m\\ge 0\\) and \\(m\\neq 0\\) unless \\(c=n-2\\), in which case \\(m=0\\) does not occur because we may instead use the pair \\((c,1)\\) and \\((c+2,n-c-2)\\); the resulting slope is \\(\\frac{n-c-3}{2}\\neq0,-1\\).  Hence we can always choose two points of \\(P_\\ell\\) whose connecting line has slope different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\).  That line is sunny.\n\n2. **Horizontal**: \\(\\ell\\) has equation \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-2\\).  \n   By symmetry we obtain a sunny line through \\((1,c)\\) and \\((n-c,c+1)\\); its slope is  \n\n   \\[\n   m=\\frac{c+1-c}{n-c-1}= \\frac{1}{n-c-1}\\neq 0,-1 .\n   \\]\n\n3. **Diagonal**: \\(\\ell\\) is \\(x+y=n\\).  \n   The extreme points are \\((1,n-1)\\) and \\((n-1,1)\\).  \n   The line through \\((1,n-1)\\) and \\((2,n-2)\\) has slope \\(-1\\) (forbidden), but the line through \\((1,n-1)\\) and \\((3,n-3)\\) has slope \\(-1+\\frac{2}{2}=0\\) (also forbidden).  \n   Taking \\((1,n-1)\\) and \\((4,n-4)\\) gives slope \\(-1+\\frac{3}{3}=0\\) again.  \n   Choose instead \\((1,n-1)\\) and \\((n-2,2)\\); the slope is  \n\n   \\[\n   m=\\frac{2-(n-1)}{(n-2)-1}= \\frac{3-n}{n-3}= -1+\\frac{2}{n-3}.\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n\\ge 4\\) this value is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(-1\\); for \\(n=3\\) the diagonal line is the only line needed and the case \\(k=0\\) has already been handled.  Hence for all \\(n\\ge3\\) we can replace the diagonal by a sunny line.\n\nIn each case we have produced a line \\(\\ell'\\) that passes through at least the two extreme points of \\(P_\\ell\\); consequently every intermediate point of \\(P_\\ell\\) (which lies on the segment joining the extremes) also belongs to \\(\\ell'\\).  Thus \\(P_\\ell\\subseteq\\ell'\\) and \\(\\ell'\\) is sunny. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Incrementing the number of sunny lines\n\nStart from the base family \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) (which has \\(0\\) sunny lines).  \nIf we wish to obtain a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)), perform the following procedure \\(k\\) times:\n\n* pick any line \\(\\ell\\) of the current family that is not sunny;\n* replace \\(\\ell\\) by the sunny line \\(\\ell'\\) constructed in Section\u202f2.\n\nEach replacement preserves the covering of \\(T_n\\) (the points formerly covered only by \\(\\ell\\) are now covered by \\(\\ell'\\)) and increases the number of sunny lines by exactly one.  After \\(k\\) steps we have a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that still covers \\(T_n\\) and contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nBecause the family always contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, we cannot obtain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines.  Hence the attainable values of \\(k\\) are exactly the integers between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n\\,\\}}\n\\]\n\nis the set of all non\u2011negative integers for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. \u220e"
