s called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.  \nThe three families of non‑sunny lines are\n\n* vertical lines \\(x = \\alpha\\);\n* horizontal lines \\(y = \\beta\\);\n* diagonals \\(x+y = \\gamma\\).\n\nOnly the diagonal family will be needed in the construction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Diagonal covering)\n\nLet \\(S\\subseteq\\mathcal P_n\\) be a set that contains at most one point from each diagonal\n\\(D_c:\\;x+y=c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{c\\mid \\exists (a,b)\\in S,\\;a+b=c\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal D=\\{D_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1,\\;c\\notin C\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\setminus S\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal D\\), and the lines of \\(\\mathcal D\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFix a diagonal \\(D_c\\).  \nIf \\(c\\notin C\\) then no point of \\(S\\) lies on \\(D_c\\); consequently all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with sum \\(c\\) are still uncovered, and the line \\(D_c\\) contains exactly those points.  \nIf \\(c\\in C\\) then the unique point of \\(S\\) on \\(D_c\\) is already covered by some other line (in our later construction it will be a sunny line), so we do not need \\(D_c\\).  \nSince the values of \\(c\\) are distinct, the lines in \\(\\mathcal D\\) are distinct. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nChoose distinct integers  \n\n\\[\n2\\le c_1<c_2<\\dots <c_k\\le n+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) set  \n\n\\[\nQ_i=(1,c_i-1)\\in\\mathcal P_n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(Q_i\\) have pairwise different sums \\(c_i\\); hence they satisfy the hypothesis of Lemma 1.\n\nDefine the sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=(c_i-2)x+1\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) passes through \\(Q_i\\) because substituting \\(x=1\\) gives \\(y=c_i-1\\).  \nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(c_i-2\\), which is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) (the latter would require \\(c_i=1\\), impossible).  Thus every \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the slopes are distinct, so the \\(L_i\\) are distinct lines.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{c_1,\\dots ,c_k\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal D=\\{D_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1,\\;c\\notin C\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma 1, the set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\setminus\\{Q_1,\\dots ,Q_k\\}\\) is covered by the diagonals in \\(\\mathcal D\\).  \nThe family \\(\\mathcal D\\) contains exactly \\((n-1+1)-k = n-k\\) lines, all non‑sunny.\n\nConsequently the collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\cup\\mathcal D\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Exhaustiveness of the range of \\(k\\)\n\n*Upper bound.*  At most \\(n\\) lines are available, so \\(k\\le n\\).  \n*Lower bound.*  Clearly \\(k\\ge 0\\).\n\nThe construction above works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\); therefore all these values are admissible, and no other value can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
