 is **not** the diagonal \\(D_c\\) can contain at most one of those points\n(Lemma 1).  Consequently, to cover all points of \\(D_c\\) without using the line\n\\(D_c\\) we would need at least \\(c-1\\) distinct lines, one for each point.\nSince \\(c-1\\ge 2\\), omitting \\(D_c\\) would require at least two lines in place of the single line \\(D_c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nThere are exactly \\(n\\) diagonals \\(D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\).\nThe diagonal \\(D_2\\) consists of the single point \\((1,1)\\); it can be replaced by any line (sunny or not) because only one line is needed to cover that point.\n\nFor every other diagonal \\(D_c\\) (\\(c\\ge 3\\)) the argument above shows that the line\n\\(D_c\\) itself must belong to any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all\npoints of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); otherwise we would need at least two lines to replace it,\nwhich would increase the total number of lines beyond \\(n\\).\n\nHence among the \\(n\\) lines at most the line that replaces \\(D_2\\) can be sunny.\nTherefore the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 1 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence of configurations for the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe lines are distinct, none is sunny, and every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on exactly one of them. Thus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n*Case \\(k=1\\).*  \nReplace the diagonal \\(D_2=\\{(1,1)\\}\\) by a sunny line through \\((1,1)\\); for concreteness choose  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; y=x .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_1=\\{L\\}\\cup\\{D_3,D_4,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_1\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely one of which is sunny, and it covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\): the point \\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(L\\); every other point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=c\\ge 3\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D_c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
