------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (a configuration with \\(n-2\\) sunny lines).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n-2}= \\{\\,\\ell_x : y=1\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,\\ell_y : x=1\\,\\}\\cup\n\\{\\,\\ell_c : x+y=c \\mid 2\\le c\\le n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof of covering.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_y\\).  \n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_x\\).  \n* Otherwise \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) imply \\(2\\le a+b\\le n\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{a+b}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n-2}\\).  \nThe lines \\(\\ell_x\\) and \\(\\ell_y\\) are parallel to the axes, so they are **not** sunny.  \nEach line \\(\\ell_c\\) has direction vector \\((1,-1)\\), i.e. it is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it **is** sunny.  \nConsequently \\(\\mathcal L_{n-2}\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines and contains precisely \\(n-2\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (reducing the number of sunny lines).  \nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing \\(s\\ge1\\) sunny lines. Then there exists another family \\(\\mathcal F'\\) with the same properties and containing exactly \\(s-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nSelect a sunny line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal F\\). Because \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to the axes, it meets the vertical line \\(x=1\\) at a point \\((1,y_0)\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) at a point \\((x_0,1)\\) (possibly \\(x_0=1\\) or \\(y_0=1\\)).  \n\nIf the point \\((1,y_0)\\) is not covered by any other line of \\(\\mathcal F\\), replace \\(\\ell\\) by the vertical line \\(\\ell' : x=1\\).  \nOtherwise replace \\(\\ell\\) by the horizontal line \\(\\ell' : y=1\\).  \n\nIn either case \\(\\ell'\\) is not sunny. Every point that was on \\(\\ell\\) remains covered because \\(\\ell'\\) passes through at least one of the two intersection points of \\(\\ell\\) with the axes, and all other lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) are unchanged. Hence the new family \\(\\mathcal F'\\) still covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and has one fewer sunny line. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nStarting from the configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n-2}\\) of Lemma 2 (which has \\(n-2\\) sunny lines), we may apply Lemma 3 repeatedly. After \\(t\\) applications we obtain a configuration with exactly \\((n-2)-t\\) sunny lines. Choosing \\(t\\) from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-2\\) yields configurations with every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved two complementary statements:\n\n* Any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition can have at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines (inequality (3)).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists such a family containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (Lemmas 2 and 3).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
