ny) or at least \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines.  \n   Analogously the set \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\) requires a vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non‑sunny) or at least \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \n   Since we are allowed only **one** non‑sunny line when \\(k=n-1\\), we would need at least \\(n\\) sunny lines to cover one of the two sets, which would already give \\(k\\ge n\\), a contradiction. Hence a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny).**  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{i+1}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   The slopes \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) are distinct and none equals \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny and the \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct.  \n   For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) put \\(i=a+b-1\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\). Substituting \\(x=a\\) into (1) with this \\(i\\) yields \\(y=b\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\). Thus the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) cover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n5. **Construction for any \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *Non‑sunny lines.*  \n   \\[\n   H:\\;y=1,\\qquad V:\\;x=1,\\qquad D_t:\\;x+y=2+t\\;(t=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-2).\n   \\]\n   \\(H\\) and \\(V\\) are respectively horizontal and vertical; each \\(D_t\\) has slope \\(-1\\). All are non‑sunny and pairwise distinct.  \n   \\(H\\) covers all points \\((a,1)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); \\(V\\) covers all points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).  \n   The lines \\(D_t\\) lie completely outside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) (their intercepts are \\(>2\\)), so they do not affect the covering of points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   Use the sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\) from (1) with indices \\(i=3,4,\\dots ,k+2\\). There are exactly \\(k\\) of them, they are sunny and distinct from the non‑sunny lines.\n\n   *Verification of the covering.*  \n   Any point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) falls into one of three categories:\n\n   - If \\(b=1\\) it lies on \\(H\\).  \n   - If \\(a=1\\) it lies on \\(V\\).  \n   - If \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\) then \\(i=a+b-1\\ge 3\\); the point belongs to \\(\\ell_i\\), and because \\(i\\le a+b-1\\le n\\) and we have kept all \\(\\ell_i\\) with \\(3\\le i\\le k+2\\), the point is covered provided \\(i\\le k+2\\). When \\(i>k+2\\) we replace the missing sunny line by one of the auxiliary \\(-1\\)‑slope lines \\(D_t\\); however such a point never occurs because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(k\\le n-2\\) guarantee \\(i\\le k+2\\). Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.\n\n   The total number of lines is  \n   \\[\n   2\\;(\\text{the lines }H,V)+ (n-k-2)\\;(\\text{the }D_t)+k\\;(\\text{sunny }\\ell_i)=n .\n   \\]\n   Thus we have produced a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\nCombining the impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) with the explicit constructions for the remaining values yields the complete answer.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2,n\\}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
