n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) can satisfy such an equation; hence \\(|\\ell\\cap\\mathcal{P}|\\le n\\).\n\nIf \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) (sunny case), then \\(\\ell\\) can be written as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nSince the points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) have integer coordinates, any two distinct points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) lying on \\(\\ell\\) determine the rational slope \\(m=\\frac{b_{2}-b_{1}}{a_{2}-a_{1}}\\).  \nBecause \\(|a_{2}-a_{1}|\\le n-1\\) and \\(|b_{2}-b_{1}|\\le n-1\\), the denominator of the reduced fraction representing \\(m\\) is at most \\(n-1\\).  \nConsequently a line of such a slope can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\); otherwise two of those points would give a denominator exceeding \\(n-1\\), a contradiction. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Global incidence inequality)\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{P}|\\le (n-1)k + n s = n^{2}-k .\n\\]\n\n**Proof.**  \nEach sunny line contributes at most \\(n-1\\) incidences with \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) by Lemma 1, and each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) incidences. Summing over all \\(k\\) sunny and \\(s\\) non‑sunny lines yields the stated bound. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequence\n\nInsert the value of \\(|\\mathcal{P}|\\) from (1) into Lemma 2:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le n^{2}-k\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk\\le \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Extremal constructions\n\n*Construction for \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) lines\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1,\\qquad y=1 .\n\\]\nAll of them are non‑sunny (vertical or horizontal).  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}\\) has \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) or \\(b=1\\); hence it lies on at least one of the chosen lines