 maximal sunny configuration (all \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\)), we replace sunny lines by suitable non\u2011sunny lines while preserving coverage.  \n   - For any integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le \\frac{n(n-1)}2\\) we construct a configuration with exactly \\(k=t\\) sunny lines.  \n   - Choose a subset \\(S\\) of \\(t\\) of the sunny lines of slope \\(1\\) and keep them.  \n   - The points not covered by the chosen sunny lines are exactly those lying on the remaining \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}2-t\\) slope\u2011\\(1\\) lines.  \n   - Those uncovered points form a union of at most \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}2-t\\) *parallel* families: each family consists of points having the same sum \\(a+b=c\\). For each such family we cover its points by a single non\u2011sunny line:\n        * if the family size is \\(1\\) we use a vertical line \\(x=a\\);\n        * if the family size is \\(2\\) we use a horizontal line \\(y=b\\);\n        * if the family size is \\(\\ge 3\\) we use a line of the form \\(x+y=c\\) (which is non\u2011sunny).  \n   - Because there are at most \\(n\\) distinct values of \\(c\\), we need at most \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines. Since the total number of lines must be exactly \\(n\\), we can always add extra non\u2011sunny lines (e.g. unused vertical lines) without affecting coverage.  \n   - Consequently we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines and a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n   This shows that every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (1) can be realised.\n\nCombining the necessary bound (1) with the constructions for the extremal and intermediate values yields the complete description of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation and basic counting\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(\\mathcal P\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nIts cardinality is\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\) be \\(n\\) distinct lines such that every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on at least one of them.  \nDenote by  \n\n* \\(k\\) \u2013 the number of *sunny* lines (slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\));\n* \\(s=n-k\\) \u2013 the number of *non\u2011sunny* lines (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Incidence bound for a single line\n\n#### Lemma 1  \n*If a line \\(\\ell\\) is sunny then \\(|\\ell\\cap\\mathcal P|\\le n-1\\);  \nif \\(\\ell\\) is non\u2011sunny then \\(|\\ell\\cap\\mathcal P|\\le n\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \n\n*Non\u2011sunny case.*  \nA non\u2011sunny line is of one of the forms  \n\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c,\n\\]\n\nwith integer \\(c\\).  \nBecause the coordinates of points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) satisfy \\(1\\le a,b\\le n\\), each of the three equations can have at most \\(n\\) integer solutions inside the triangle; hence \\(|\\ell\\cap\\mathcal P|\\le n\\).\n\n*Sunny case.*  \nWrite a sunny line as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nIf two distinct points \\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2})\\in\\mathcal P\\) lie on \\(\\ell\\), then  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{b_{2}-b_{1}}{a_{2}-a_{1}} .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(1\\le a_{i},b_{i}\\le n\\), we have \\(|a_{2}-a_{1}|\\le n-1\\) and \\(|b_{2}-b_{1}|\\le n-1\\).  \nThus, after reducing the fraction, the denominator of \\(m\\) does not exceed \\(n-1\\).  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) contained \\(n\\) distinct points of \\(\\mathcal P\\), then among those points there would be two with a difference in the \\(x\\)-coordinate equal to \\(n-1\\), forcing the denominator of \\(m\\) to be at least \\(n\\), a contradiction.  \nHence \\(|\\ell\\cap\\mathcal P|\\le n-1\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Global incidence inequality\n\nEach sunny line contributes at most \\(n-1\\) incidences with \\(\\mathcal P\\), each non\u2011sunny line at most \\(n\\). Summing over all \\(n\\) lines gives\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|\\le (n-1)k + n s = (n-1)k + n(n-k)=n^{2}-k . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nInsert (2) into (3):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le n^{2}-k\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk\\le \\frac{n(n-1)}2 . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le \\frac{n(n-1)}2 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Extremal constructions\n\n#### 4.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-1,\\qquad y=1 .\n\\]\n\nAll are non\u2011sunny (vertical or horizontal).  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) either \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) (so the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\)) or \\(b=1\\) (so it lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\)).  \nHence every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is covered and the configuration uses exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 4.2  The case \\(k=\\dfrac{n(n-1)}2\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) consider the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_{c}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\mid a+b=c\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal P_{c}\\) consists of \\(c-1\\) points lying on the line \\(x+y=c\\) (slope \\(-1\\)), but we will use *different* lines of slope \\(1\\) that pass through pairs of points of \\(\\mathcal P_{c}\\).  \nAny two distinct points of \\(\\mathcal P_{c}\\) determine a line of slope \\(1\\); distinct unordered pairs give distinct lines because two different pairs have different mid\u2011points.  \nHence the number of such lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}\\binom{c-1}{2}\n= \\binom{n}{2}\n= \\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nAll these lines have slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore they are sunny.  \nEach point of \\(\\mathcal P_{c}\\) lies on exactly \\(c-2 = n-1\\) of the lines of slope \\(1\\) coming from \\(\\mathcal P_{c}\\), so every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is incident to at least one of the constructed lines.  \nThus a configuration with \\(k_{\\max}= \\frac{n(n-1)}2\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Construction for an arbitrary admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(t\\) be any integer satisfying \\(0\\le t\\le \\frac{n(n-1)}2\\).  \nWe shall build a configuration with exactly \\(k=t\\) sunny lines.\n\n1. **Start from the maximal sunny family.**  \n   Let \\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{sunny}}\\) be the set of all \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\) described in \u00a74.2.\n\n2. **Select \\(t\\) sunny lines to keep.**  \n   Choose an arbitrary subset \\(S\\subset\\mathcal L_{\\text{sunny}}\\) with \\(|S|=t\\).  \n   Keep these lines in the final configuration.\n\n3. **Identify the points still uncovered.**  \n   A point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) is uncovered precisely when *none* of the lines of \\(S\\) passes through it.  \n   Because all lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{sunny}}\\) have slope \\(1\\), the uncovered points are exactly those belonging to those \u201csum\u2011levels\u2019\u2019 \\(c=a+b\\) for which we have discarded *all* lines of slope \\(1\\) that involve the point.  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) the set \\(\\mathcal P_{c}\\) contains \\(c-1\\) points; the lines of slope \\(1\\) inside \\(\\mathcal P_{c}\\) are \\(\\binom{c-1}{2}\\) in number.  \n   By discarding some of them we may completely eliminate the coverage of \\(\\mathcal P_{c}\\) only if we discard **all** \\(\\binom{c-1}{2}\\) lines belonging to that level.\n\n   Consequently the uncovered points are a union of whole levels \\(\\mathcal P_{c}\\) for which we have removed *all* corresponding slope\u2011\\(1\\) lines.\n\n4. **Cover each uncovered level by a single non\u2011sunny line.**  \n   For a level \\(\\mathcal P_{c}\\) we have three possibilities:\n\n   *If \\(|\\mathcal P_{c}|=1\\)* (i.e. \\(c=2\\) or \\(c=n+1\\)), cover the single point by a vertical line \\(x=a\\) (or a horizontal line \\(y=b\\)).  \n\n   *If \\(|\\mathcal P_{c}|=2\\)* (i.e. \\(c=3\\) or \\(c=n\\)), the two points have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate or the same \\(y\\)-coordinate; a vertical or horizontal line again suffices.  \n\n   *If \\(|\\mathcal P_{c}|\\ge 3\\)*, the points all satisfy \\(a+b=c\\); the line \\(x+y=c\\) (which is non\u2011sunny because its slope is \\(-1\\)) contains the whole level.\n\n   Thus each uncovered level can be covered by **one** non\u2011sunny line.\n\n5. **Count the needed non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   There are at most \\(n\\) distinct values of \\(c\\) (namely \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\)), so at most \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines are required.  \n   The total number of lines in the configuration is therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   |S| \\;+\\; (\\text{number of uncovered levels}) \\;\\le\\; t + n .\n   \\]\n\n   If this sum is smaller than \\(n\\) we simply add arbitrary extra non\u2011sunny lines (e.g. unused vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots\\)) until we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \n   Adding such lines does not affect the covering property.\n\n6. **Result.**  \n   The final family consists of the \\(t\\) chosen sunny lines together with a suitable collection of non\u2011sunny lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines, and it covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  \n   Hence a configuration with exactly \\(k=t\\) sunny lines exists for every integer \\(t\\) satisfying (5).\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* The necessary condition \\(k\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\) (inequality (4)).\n* The existence of configurations attaining the two extreme values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\).\n* A general construction that, for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\), produces a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0,1,2,\\dots ,\\dfrac{n(n-1)}2\\; } .\n\\] \u220e"
