2}\\) distinct sunny lines.  \n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) satisfies \\(a<b\\) or \\(a=b\\) or \\(a>b\\).  \n   - If \\(a<b\\) set \\(i=a,\\;j=b\\); then \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_{i,j}\\).  \n   - If \\(a=b\\) set \\(i=a,\\;j=a+1\\); then \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_{i,j}\\) because \\((a,b)=(i, i)\\) lies on the line \\(y=x+(j-i)=x+1\\) after shifting the parameter \\(t\\).  \n   - If \\(a>b\\) we use the symmetric pair \\((b,a)\\) and the same argument.  \n   Hence every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(\\ell_{i,j}\\).  \n   This gives a configuration with exactly \\(\\dfrac{n(n-1)}{2}\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Intermediate values.**  \n   Let \\(t\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le t\\le \\dfrac{n(n-1)}{2}\\).  \n   \u2013 Start from the maximal sunny family \\(\\mathcal L_{\\max}=\\{\\ell_{i,j}\\}\\).  \n   \u2013 Choose an arbitrary subset \\(S\\subset\\mathcal L_{\\max}\\) with \\(|S|=t\\) and keep these sunny lines.  \n   \u2013 The points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) not covered by \\(S\\) lie on the remaining lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{\\max}\\).  \n   \u2013 For each value \\(c\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) consider the \u201csum\u2011level\u2019\u2019  \n\n     \\[\n     \\mathcal P_{c}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\mid a+b=c\\}.\n     \\]\n\n     All points of a given level have the same sum \\(c\\).  \n     If a level \\(\\mathcal P_{c}\\) is completely uncovered (i.e. we have discarded **all** lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{\\max}\\) that contain any point of that level), we cover the whole level by a single non\u2011sunny line:\n     * if \\(|\\mathcal P_{c}|=1\\) we use a vertical line \\(x=a\\);\n     * if \\(|\\mathcal P_{c}|=2\\) we use a horizontal line \\(y=b\\);\n     * if \\(|\\mathcal P_{c}|\\ge3\\) we use the line \\(x+y=c\\) (slope \\(-1\\), non\u2011sunny).\n\n     Because there are at most \\(n\\) different values of \\(c\\), at most \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines are needed.\n\n   \u2013 Add any further non\u2011sunny lines (e.g. unused