"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified the gaps pointed out in the bug report and replaced the faulty parts with rigorous arguments.  \nThe complete solution shows that for a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,\\;1,\\;2,\\;\\dots ,\\;n } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Incidence Lemma.**  \n   *Lemma\u202f1.*  \n   \u2013 A sunny line can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points of the set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   \u2013 A non\u2011sunny line (i.e. a line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n   The proof uses the fact that the difference of the \\(x\\)-coordinates of any two points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is at most \\(n-1\\); a sunny line with slope \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\) in lowest terms would have \\(|q|\\le n-1\\), hence it can meet \\(\\mathcal P\\) in at most \\(n-1\\) points.  For a vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\) line the same elementary counting gives the bound \\(n\\).\n\n2. **Necessary condition.**  \n   From Lemma\u202f1, if a configuration contains \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, then  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal P|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n   \\le k\\,(n-1)+(n-k)\\,n\n   =n^{2}-k .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Of course \\(k\\ge 0\\).  Thus any feasible \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Extremal constructions.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   together with the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) has either \\(a\\le n-1\\) (hence lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\)) or \\(a=n\\); in the latter case the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\), so the point lies on \\(y=1\\).  All lines are non\u2011sunny, giving a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n   *\\(k=n\\).*  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = x + i .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(1\\), which is not parallel to any forbidden direction, so each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \n   The points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) that lie on \\(\\ell_i\\) are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   (a,b)=(t,\\;t+i),\\qquad\n   t=1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(t+i\\ge 2\\) and \\(t+(t+i)=2t+i\\le n+1\\) by the choice of the upper bound, every pair \\((t,t+i)\\) indeed belongs to \\(\\mathcal P\\).  \n   Moreover, for any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) we have \\(b-a\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\); setting \\(i=b-a\\) and \\(t=a\\) we obtain \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_i\\).  Hence the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) cover the whole set \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n4. **Intermediate values \\(0<k<n\\).**  \n\n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n   *Sunny part.*  Take the first \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) defined in (6).  \n   *Non\u2011sunny part.*  To cover the points that are not already covered, we use the following \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n   * the vertical line \\(x=1\\);\n   * the vertical line \\(x=2\\);\n   * \u2026;\n   * the vertical line \\(x=n-k\\).\n\n   (If \\(n-k=1\\) this is just the single line \\(x=1\\); if \\(n-k>1\\) we have exactly \\(n-k\\) distinct vertical lines.)\n\n   **Verification of coverage.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\).  \n   - If \\(b-a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_{b-a}\\) by (7).  \n   - Otherwise \\(b-a\\ge k+1\\).  Since \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\ge a+(k+1)\\), we have \\(a\\le n-(k+1)\\).  Consequently \\(a\\) belongs to the set \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\), and the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which is among the chosen non\u2011sunny lines) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is covered by at least one of the \\(k\\) sunny lines and the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines.  The total number of distinct lines is exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\), and precisely \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   Hence for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (4) a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition (4) is also sufficient, because we have exhibited explicit constructions for the extremal cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) and a uniform construction for every intermediate \\(k\\).  Therefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}} .\n   \\] \u220e"

