            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Incidence bounds)\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider a line \\(\\ell\\) that is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nIts equation can be written as  \n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{p}{q}x + c ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\; q\\neq 0,\\; p\\neq -q .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIf \\((x_0,y_0)\\in\\mathcal P\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\), then every other integer point of \\(\\ell\\) has the form  \n\n\\[\n(x_0+mq,\\;y_0+mp),\\qquad m\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) satisfy \\(x+y\\le n+1\\), the quantity  \n\n\\[\n(x_0+mq)+(y_0+mp)=x_0+y_0+m(p+q)\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nmust stay \\(\\le n+1\\).  Since \\(p+q\\neq 0\\) (otherwise the line would have slope \\(-1\\)), the absolute value \\(|p+q|\\ge 1\\).  Starting from the smallest admissible \\(m\\) and increasing \\(m\\) by one raises the sum in (9) by at least \\(1\\); consequently at most \\((n+1)-(x_0+y_0)\\) further points can appear on the same line.  Because \\(x_0+y_0\\ge 2\\), we obtain the bound  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(\\ell\\cap\\mathcal P\\bigr)\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-coordinate is constant; the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) then allows at most \\(n\\) different integer \\(x\\)-values, so \\(|\\ell\\cap\\mathcal P|\\le n\\).  The same argument works for a vertical line and for a line of slope \\(-1\\).  ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Necessary condition\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(\\mathcal P\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(n-1\\) points, each non‑sunny line at most \\(n\\) points; therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 = |\\mathcal P|\n   \\le k\\,(n-1)+(n-k)\\,n = n^{2}-k .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(k\\le n\\).  Since a number of lines cannot be negative, any feasible \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Constructions\n\nWe now show that every integer \\(k\\) obeying (11) can actually be realised.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nand the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) and \\(a\\le n-1\\) then the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \nIf \\(a=n\\) then the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\), so the point lies on \\(y=1\\).  \nAll lines are non‑sunny, giving a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = x + i .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(1\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) we have \\(b-a\\ge 1\\) (otherwise \\(a=b\\) would give \\(2a\\le n+1\\) and consequently \\(b-a=0\\) would contradict the definition of \\(\\mathcal P\\)).  \nSetting \\(i=b-a\\) we obtain \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_i\\).  Hence the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) cover \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  General \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{Sunny lines: } &&\\ell_i:\\; y = x + i ,\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,k ,\\\\\n&\\text{Non‑sunny lines: } &&v_j:\\; x=j ,\\qquad j=1,\\dots ,n-k .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are distinct; the \\(\\ell_i\\) are sunny, the \\(v_j\\) are vertical and therefore non‑sunny.\n\n*Coverage proof.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*If \\(b-a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_{\\,b-a}\\) by definition.\n\n*If \\(b-a\\le 0\\)*, then \\(b\\le a\\).  From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n2a\\le n+1+(a-b)\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(a\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le n-k\\) (the last inequality holds because \\(k\\le n-1\\) implies \\(n-k\\ge 1\\) and \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\le n-k\\) for every admissible \\(k\\)).  Consequently the vertical line \\(v_a\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*If \\(b-a\\ge k+1\\)*, write \\(b-a=k+t\\) with \\(t\\ge 1\\).  The inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n2a + k + t \\le n+1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a \\le n-k-t .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t\\ge 1\\), we have \\(a\\le n-k-1\\le n-k\\); again \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(v_a\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) belongs to at least one line of the family (14).  The family consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, i.e. of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition (11) and the constructions for the three cases together prove that for a 