            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Incidence bounds)\n\n*Statement.*  \n\n* (i) If a line \\(\\ell\\) is sunny, then \\(|\\ell\\cap\\mathcal P|\\le n-1\\).  \n* (ii) If \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\), then \\(|\\ell\\cap\\mathcal P|\\le n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non‑vertical line can be written as  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{p}{q}x+c ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;q\\neq0 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nIf \\((x_0,y_0)\\in\\mathcal P\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\), then every integer point of \\(\\ell\\) has the form  \n\n\\[\n(x_0+mq,\\;y_0+mp),\\qquad m\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe sum of the coordinates of such a point equals  \n\n\\[\n(x_0+y_0)+m(p+q).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nBecause all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) satisfy \\(x+y\\le n+1\\), the integer \\(m\\) can vary only while keeping the sum in (11) at most \\(n+1\\).  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is sunny then \\(p+q\\neq0\\) (otherwise the slope would be \\(-1\\)).  \nHence the absolute step \\(|p+q|\\) is