            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Incidence bounds)\n\n*Statement.*  \n\n1. If a line \\(\\ell\\) is sunny, then \\(|\\ell\\cap\\mathcal P|\\le n-1\\).  \n2. If \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\), then \\(|\\ell\\cap\\mathcal P|\\le n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite a non‑vertical line as \\(y=\\frac{p}{q}x+c\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) (\\(q\\neq0\\)).  \nAll integer points on this line are \\((x_0+mq,\\;y_0+mp)\\) for some integer \\(m\\).  \nThe sum of the coordinates of such a point equals \\((x_0+y_0)+m(p+q)\\).  \n\nFor a sunny line we have \\(p+q\\neq0\\) (otherwise the slope would be \\(-1\\)).  \nHence the sum changes by at least \\(|p+q|\\ge1\\) when \\(m\\) varies by \\(1\\).  \nSince every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) satisfies \\(x+y\\le n+1\\) and \\(x+y\\ge2\\), the integer \\(m\\) can take at most \\(n-1\\) different values, giving at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) on \\(\\ell\\). This proves (1).\n\nIf \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal, say \\(y=c\\), then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n+1-c\\); thus at most \\(n\\) integer values of \\(a\\) are possible.  \nThe same argument works for a vertical line \\(x=c\\) and for a line of slope \\(-1\\) (equation \\(x+y=c\\)).  Hence (2). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Necessary condition on the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(\\mathcal P\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nUsing Lemma 1 we have  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|\n   \\le k\\,(n-1)+(n-k)\\,n = n^{2}-k .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>0\\), the inequality forces \\(k\\le n\\).  \nTogether with the obvious lower bound \\(k\\ge0\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus any feasible \\(k\\) must satisfy (4).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Constructions attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe now show that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nand the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) and \\(a\\le n-1\\) then the point lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nIf \\(a=n\\) then the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\), so the point lies on \\(y=1\\).  \nAll lines are non‑sunny, giving a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = x + i .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(1\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) we have \\(0\\le b-a\\le n-1\\) (the upper bound follows from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  \nChoosing \\(i=b-a\\) yields \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_i\\).  Thus the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_0,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\) cover \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The case \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Sunny lines}}\\qquad\n\\ell_i:\\; y = x + i ,\\qquad i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Non‑sunny lines}}\\qquad\n\\sigma_j:\\; x + y = j ,\\qquad j=2,3,\\dots ,n-k+1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe \\(\\ell_i\\) are sunny (slope \\(1\\)); the \\(\\sigma_j\\) are parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny.  \nThere are exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nWe verify that every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on at least one of the lines (7)–(8).\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Case A.* \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the sunny line \\(\\ell_{\\,b-a}\\).\n\n*Case B.* \\(b-a\\ge k\\).  \nWrite \\(b-a = k+t\\) with \\(t\\ge0\\).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n2a + k + t \\le n+1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a \\le n-k-t .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t\\ge0\\), (9) gives \\(a\\le n-k\\).  \nConsequently the sum \\(a+b\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\na+b = a + (a+k+t) = 2a + k + t \\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nMoreover \\(a+b\\ge 2\\) and, because \\(a\\le n-k\\), we have  \n\n\\[\na+b \\le (n-k) + (n-k + k) = n-k+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(a+b\\) is an integer between \\(2\\) and \\(n-k+1\\); the line \\(\\sigma_{\\,a+b}\\) (which is of the form \\(x+y=j\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*Case C.* \\(b-a\\le -1\\) (i.e. \\(b<a\\)).  \nThen \\(a-b\\ge1\\).  From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we get  \n\n\\[\n2b + (a-b) \\le n+1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad b \\le n - (a-b) .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a-b\\ge1\\), we have \\(b\\le n-1\\).  Moreover \\(b\\ge1\\), so \\(b\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\).  \nIf \\(b\\