ontains exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Replacing a vertical line by a sunny line\n\nFix an integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-1\\).  \nThe vertical line \\(x=t\\) contains the points  \n\n\\[\nV_t=\\{(t,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-t\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nM_t:\\;y=-(t-1)x+(n+1-t).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(M_t\\) passes through the two extreme points of \\(V_t\\):\n\\[\n(t,1)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad(t,n+1-t).\n\\]\nSince its slope \\(-(t-1)\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\), the line \\(M_t\\) is sunny.\n\nWe claim that after deleting the vertical line \\(x=t\\) and inserting the sunny line \\(M_t\\) the covering property of the whole family is preserved.\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nEvery point of \\(V_t\\) other than the two extremes has coordinates \\((t,b)\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-t\\).  \nFor such a point we have  \n\n\\[\n(t,b) = (t-1,b+1) + (1,-1) = (t+1,b-1) + (-1,1).\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\((t,b)\\) lies on the same diagonal \\(x+y = (t-1)+(b+1) = t+b\\) as the point \\((t-1,b+1)\\) and also on the diagonal \\(x+y = (t+1)+(b-1) = t+b\\) as the point \\((t+1,b-1)\\).  \nHence the point \\((t,b)\\) is covered simultaneously by the two neighbouring vertical lines \\(x=t-1\\) and \\(x=t+1\\).  \nTherefore after the replacement all points of \\(S_n\\) remain covered. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Obtaining any number \\(k\\) of sunny lines with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nStart from the all‑vertical configuration (3).  \nChoose an arbitrary subset \\(T\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with \\(|T|=k\\).  \nFor each \\(t\\in T\\) replace the line \\(x=t\\) by the sunny line \\(M_t\\) defined in (5).  \nBy the claim of Section 2, after all replacements the resulting family still covers \\(S_n\\).  \nExactly the lines indexed by \\(T\\) are sunny, so the family contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny l