nes that still pass through the points originally covered by the replaced lines preserves the covering condition.  \n   - Because we can select the sunny lines with arbitrary distinct slopes, we obtain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for any prescribed \\(k\\).\n\nHence every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is realizable.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\binom{n+1}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Covering by at most \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines)\n\nThere exists a set \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) of at most \\(n\\) lines, each of which is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\), such that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe construct \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) greedily.\n\n1. **Horizontal lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) consider the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \n   This line contains exactly the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\).  \n   Hence the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) covers all points of \\(S_n\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(b\\).\n\n2. **Vertical lines.**  \n   Analogously, for each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) the vertical line \\(x=a\\) covers all points of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(a\\).\n\n3. **Diagonal lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) the line \\(x+y=c\\) contains exactly the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=c\\) and \\(a,b\\ge1\\).\n\nObserve that the three families together contain exactly the three “directions’’ needed to pass through any point of \\(S_n\\). Moreover, the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\}\\cup\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\}\\cup\\{x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nhas cardinality \\(3n\\). To reduce this to at most \\(n\\) we not