ost \\(n\\) we note that many of these lines are redundant.  \n\nIndeed, the points of \\(S_n\\) can be partitioned into the \\(n\\) \u201cantidiagonals\u2019\u2019  \n\n\\[\nA_j=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=j+1\\},\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,n .\n\\]\n\nEach antidiagonal \\(A_j\\) is a set of collinear points lying on the line \\(x+y=j+1\\).  \nConsequently the \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\;x+y=j+1\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nalready cover all points of \\(S_n\\). Hence we may take  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich consists of exactly \\(n\\) lines, all parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (hence non\u2011sunny). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Insertion of sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be any collection of \\(n\\) lines that covers \\(S_n\\).  \nFor any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a collection \\(\\mathcal L'\\) of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(S_n\\) such that exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L'\\) are sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart from the covering \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) given by Lemma\u202f1, which consists of \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \nChoose any subset \\(T\\subseteq\\mathcal L_0\\) with \\(|T|=k\\).  \nFor each line \\(\\ell\\in T\\) pick a slope \\(\\sigma_\\ell\\) different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and distinct for different \\(\\ell\\).  \nBecause each \\(\\ell\\) contains at least one point of \\(S_n\\), we can select a point \\((a_\\ell,b_\\ell)\\in\\ell\\cap S_n\\) and define the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell'_{\\sigma_\\ell}:\\; y-b_\\ell=\\sigma_\\ell (x-a_\\ell).\n\\]\n\nBy construction \\(\\ell'_{\\sigma_\\ell}\\) passes through the same point \\((a_\\ell,b_\\ell)\\) that \\(\\ell\\) covered, and it is sunny because its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\).  \n\nReplace each \\(\\ell\\in T\\) by the corresponding sunny line \\(\\ell'_{\\sigma_\\ell}\\).  \nThe resulting family \\(\\mathcal L'\\) still contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, still covers every point of \\(S_n\\) (each point that was covered by a replaced line is still covered by the new sunny line through the same point), and now has precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem (Admissible values of \\(k\\))\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma\u202f1 provides a covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines, showing that \\(k=0\\) is attainable.  \nLemma\u202f2 shows that, starting from any covering by \\(n\\) lines, we can replace an arbitrary number \\(k\\) of its lines by sunny lines while preserving the covering property.  \nHence for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nWe have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) each integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n\n\\]\n\ncan be realized by a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nThus the set of admissible \\(k\\) contains the whole interval \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  Whether additional restrictions on \\(k\\) exist (i.e. whether some values in this interval might be impossible) remains an open question."
