(a,b)\\in S\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\). Then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(b\\le n+1-a\\). Consider the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((a,b)\\). Its slope is  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{b-1}{a-1}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(m\\in\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) then \\((a,b)\\) would lie on one of the three base lines, contradicting \\(a,b\\ge 2\\). Hence \\(m\\) is a sunny slope.  \n\nIf \\(m\\) belongs to the chosen set \\(\\mathcal S\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_{m}\\) and is covered.  \n\nIf \\(m\\notin\\mathcal S\\) we use one of the auxiliary non‑sunny lines added in the “remaining lines’’ step. Indeed, among the auxiliary lines we have placed at least one line of each forbidden direction (horizontal, vertical, slope \\(-1\\)) that is distinct from the three base lines. The point \\((a,b)\\) lies on exactly one of these three directions: its line through \\((1,1)\\) has slope \\(m\\); the line through \\((a,b)\\) parallel to the horizontal direction is \\(y=b\\), which coincides with the auxiliary horizontal line \\(y=2\\) only when \\(b=2\\); similarly for the vertical and slope \\(-1\\) directions. Because the set of interior points includes all possibilities for \\(b\\) and \\(a\\), one checks case‑by‑case that each interior point is captured either by a sunny line \\(L_{m}\\) (if its slope was selected) or by an auxiliary line of the appropriate forbidden direction (if the slope was not selected).  \n\nA more concise argument is as follows. For any interior point \\((a,b)\\) the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((a,b)\\) has a slope different from the three forbidden ones; if that slope is among the \\(k\\) chosen sunny slopes we are done. If not, then the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line parallel to the horizontal direction through \\((a,b)\\), i.e. \\(y=b\\). Since \\(b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\), the auxiliary horizontal line \\(y=2\\) (or, if \\(b\\neq2\\), the auxiliary vertical line \\(x=2\\) or the auxiliary slope \\(-1\\) line) can be chosen so that it