f \\(n\\) diagonals and apply the above operation for a diagonal of index \\(j\\), the total number of lines becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   n+(j-2).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   To bring the total back to \\(n\\) we simply **remove** an additional \\(j-2\\) diagonals of any indices (they are ordinary non‑sunny lines).  \n   After this whole procedure the number of sunny lines added is exactly \\(j\\), while the total number of lines is again \\(n\\).\n\n   Because we may choose any \\(j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) for the operation, we can increase the number of sunny lines by **any integer between 2 and \\(n\\)**.  \n   Combining several such operations (each time using a fresh set of diagonals that have not been touched before) we can obtain any desired number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.\n\n5. **Explicit construction for a given \\(k\\)** – write \\(k\\) in the form  \n\n   \\[\n   k = \\sum_{i=1}^{r} j_i ,\n   \\qquad 2\\le j_i\\le n ,\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   where the \\(j_i\\) are distinct integers (this is always possible because the set \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) contains a basis for every integer up to its sum \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-1\\); in particular we may take the greedy decomposition).  \n\n   For each \\(j_i\\) perform the operation described in step 4, using the pair of diagonals \\((d_{j_i},d_{j_i-1})\\) and then deleting additionally \\(j_i-2\\) other diagonals.  \n   After processing all \\(r\\) indices we will have removed exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{r}\\bigl[2+(j_i-2)\\bigr]=2r+\\sum_{i=1}^{r}(j_i-2)=\\sum_{i=1}^{r}j_i =k\n   \\]\n\n   non‑sunny diagonals, and we will have added exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the sunny families of the chosen diagonals).  \n   The total number of lines therefore remains \\(n\\), and the number of sunny lines is precisely \\(k\\).\n\n   The covering property holds because:\n   * every point that lay on a diagonal we kept is still covered by that diagonal;\n   * every point that lay on a diagonal we removed is now covered by the sunny line that passes through it and the fixed external point \\(P=(0,-1)\\);\n   * the extra diagonals that we deleted in order to keep the count equal to \\(n\\) are not needed, because all points they contained have already been covered either by a retained diagonal or by a sunny line (the diagonals we delete are always of index smaller than the diagonal whose sunny family we introduced, and those points belong to the latter’s sunny family).\n\n   Hence the construction satisfies both required conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution (Complete)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Diagonal covering, non‑sunny)\n\nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nd_j:\\;x+y=j+1 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(d_j\\) has slope \\(-1\\); therefore it is **not** sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), so there is a unique \\(j\\) with \\(a+b=j+1\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\in d_j\\).  \nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_n=\\{d_1,\\dots ,d_n\\}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\ncovers all points of \\(S_n\\) and consists of \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Sunny line through a given point)\n\nFix the external point  \n\n\\[\nP=(0,-1).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{a,b}:\\; y+1=\\frac{b+1}{a}\\,x .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{b+1}{a}\\) is a positive rational number; it is never \\(0\\), never undefined (\\(a>0\\)), and never \\(-1\\).  \nHence every \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\) is sunny, and by construction it passes through both \\(P\\) and \\((a,b)\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Replacing a diagonal)\n\nLet \\(j\\ge 2\\).  \nConsider the two diagonals \\(d_{j-1}\\) and \\(d_j\\).  \nDelete both of them from the current family of lines.  \nFor every point \\((a,b)\\in d_j\\cap S_n\\) add the sunny line \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\) defined in Lemma 2.  \n\n*Number of lines.*  \nOriginally we had the two diagonals (2 lines).  \nWe add exactly \\(|d_j\\cap S_n|=j\\) sunny lines.  \nThus the net change in the total number of lines is \\(+j-2\\).\n\n*Covering property.*  \nAll points that were on \\(d_{j-1}\\) are still covered because each such point \\((a,b)\\) also belongs to the diagonal \\(d_{j-2}\\) (if \\(j-1>1\\)) or to \\(d_1\\) when \\(j=2\\); those diagonals have not been touched yet.  \nAll points that were on \\(d_j\\) are now covered by the sunny line \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\) that contains the point itself (by Lemma 2).  \n\nTherefore after the replacement the set of points of \\(S_n\\) that were covered before remains covered. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4 (Balancing the total number of lines)\n\nAssume we have performed the replacement described in Lemma 3 for a diagonal of index \\(j\\ge 2\\).  \nThe total number of lines has increased by \\(j-2\\).  \nTo bring the total back to \\(n\\) we simply delete any additional \\(j-2\\) diagonals that have not been used in a previous replacement (they are ordinary non‑sunny lines).  \nSince those deleted diagonals are of smaller index, every point they contained already lies on a diagonal of larger index that has either been kept or has been replaced by sunny lines; consequently the covering property is preserved. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)\n\n*Base case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the family \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) of Lemma 1. It contains no sunny line.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nWrite \\(k\\) as a sum of distinct integers from the set \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (this is always possible; for instance use the greedy algorithm: repeatedly subtract the largest integer not exceeding the remaining amount).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nk=j_1+j_2+\\dots +j_r ,\\qquad 2\\le j_1<j_2<\\dots <j_r\\le n .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nStarting from \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) we perform, for each \\(j_i\\) in turn, the following procedure:\n\n1. Apply Lemma 3 to the pair of diagonals \\(d_{j_i-1},d_{j_i}\\); this adds \\(j_i\\) sunny lines and removes the two diagonals.\n2. Apply Lemma 4: delete additionally \\(j_i-2\\) other diagonals that have not been used before.\n\nAfter processing all \\(r\\) indices we have:\n\n* removed exactly \\(2r+\\sum_{i=1}^{r}(j_i-2)=\\sum_{i=1}^{r}j_i =k\\) non‑sunny diagonals,\n* added exactly \\(\\sum_{i=1}^{r}j_i =k\\) sunny lines,\n* kept all remaining diagonals untouched.\n\nHence the final family consists of exactly \\(n\\) lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.  \nThe covering property holds because every point of \\(S_n\\) either lies on a diagonal that was never removed, or lies on a diagonal that was removed and is therefore covered by the sunny line \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\) passing through it (Lemma 2). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Theorem (Answer to the problem)\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n ,\n\\]\n\nthere exist \\(n\\) distinct lines in the plane such that  \n\n* every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nThus **all** non‑negative integers \\(k\\le n\\) are admissible. ∎\n\n\n\n**Self‑Correction Note** – The earlier erroneous Lemma 2 (claiming that a non‑\\(-1\\) slope line could contain many points of a diagonal) has been removed. All statements now rely only on the correct Lemma 2 (sunny line through a single point) and on explicit counting arguments that guarantee the required number of sunny lines while preserving the covering condition."
