ere exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing at least one sunny line.  \nLet \\(r\\ge 1\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\); consequently \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains \\(n-r\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nBecause a non\u2011sunny line must be parallel either to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or to \\(x+y=0\\), the only non\u2011sunny lines that can belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\) and still cover points of \\(S_n\\) are the diagonals \\(d_j\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of some subset \\(\\mathcal D\\subseteq\\mathcal D_n\\) of the diagonals together with the \\(r\\) sunny lines, and  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal D|+r=n .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(j_0\\) be an index such that \\(d_{j_0}\\notin\\mathcal D\\) (such a \\(j_0\\) exists because \\(r\\ge 1\\)).  \nIf \\(j_0\\le n-1\\), Lemma\u202f3 provides two distinct points of \\(S_n\\) on \\(d_{j_0}\\), namely \\((1,j_0)\\) and \\((j_0,1)\\).\n\nBy Lemma\u202f2 each sunny line meets \\(d_{j_0}\\) in at most one point.  \nHence among the \\(r\\) sunny lines at most one of the two points \\((1,j_0)\\), \\((j_0,1)\\) can be covered.  \nThe other point lies on no line of \\(\\mathcal L\\), contradicting the covering requirement.\n\nIf the omitted diagonal is \\(d_n\\) (the case \\(j_0=n\\)), then \\(d_n\\) contains the single point \\((1,n)\\).  \nAgain, at most one sunny line can pass through \\((1,n)\\); if none does, the point is uncovered, and if exactly one does, that sunny line has slope \\(\\frac{n+1}{1}>0\\neq -1\\), so it is indeed sunny, but then we have used one of the \\(r\\) sunny lines to cover this point and still have \\(r-1\\ge 0\\) remaining sunny lines.  \nHowever, the total number of lines is \\(n\\); removing \\(d_n\\) reduces the count of diagonals to \\(n-1\\), so we must have \\(r=1\\).  \nIn this situation the family consists of the \\(n-1\\) diagonals \\(d_1,\\dots ,d_{n-1}\\) and a single sunny line \\(\\ell\\) that passes through \\((1,n)\\).  \nBut \\(\\ell\\) meets each diagonal \\(d_j\\) (\\(j\\le n-1\\)) in exactly one point (Lemma\u202f2), leaving all other points of those diagonals uncovered (each diagonal contains at least two points by Lemma\u202f3).  \nThus the covering condition fails again.\n\nIn every possible case the assumption that a sunny line is present leads to a contradiction.  \nTherefore **no** family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition can contain a sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Theorem (Answer to the problem)\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the only non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\n*Existence*: the family \\(\\mathcal D_n=\\{d_1,\\dots ,d_n\\}\\) from Lemma\u202f1 contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, all of which are non\u2011sunny, and it covers every point of \\(S_n\\).  \n\n*Uniqueness*: Lemma\u202f4 shows that any family containing at least one sunny line cannot cover all points of \\(S_n\\). Hence \\(k\\ge 1\\) is impossible. \u220e"

