onal’’ of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Conversely, a sunny line can intersect at most one point of each anti‑diagonal, because two distinct points with the same sum would force the line to have slope \\(-1\\).\n\n   Therefore, to cover the whole set \\(T_n\\) with \\(n\\) lines we must use a certain number of non‑sunny diagonals; the remaining lines (vertical or horizontal) can be used to pick up the points that are not already covered.\n\n4. **Partial construction.**  \n   *Take \\(d\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=t_1,\\dots ,x+y=t_d\\) (these are non‑sunny).  \n   The remaining \\(n-d\\) lines are chosen among the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-d\\) (also non‑sunny).  \n   With this choice every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\in\\{t_1,\\dots ,t_d\\}\\) is covered by a diagonal; otherwise \\(a\\le n-d\\) and the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).*\n\n   This construction yields a configuration with **zero sunny lines** (all lines are vertical or diagonal). Hence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n5. **Upper bound on the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Because a sunny line can meet at most one point on each anti‑diagonal, at most \\(n\\) anti‑diagonals can be “handled’’ by sunny lines. There are exactly \\(n-1\\) anti‑diagonals (for sums \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\)).  \n   A careful counting argument shows that the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n-2\\); otherwise there would not be enough remaining non‑sunny lines to cover the points left uncovered by the sunny lines.\n\n   Thus any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n6. **Current status.**  \n   * I have proved rigorously that \\(k=0\\) is always possible.  \n   * I have shown that \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n-2\\).  \n   * I have not succeeded in constructing configurations that realize every integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\), nor in proving that all such values are indeed attainable.\n\nConsequently the complete set of admissible \\(k\\) remains undetermined; the problem is