of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Step 1 \u2013 two non\u2011sunny lines.*  \nTake the vertical line \\(V:x=1\\) and the horizontal line \\(H:y=1\\).\nBoth are non\u2011sunny and together they cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first\nor second coordinate equals \\(1\\).\n\n*Step 2 \u2013 the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines.*  \nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) consider the lattice point  \n\n\\[\nP_t=(t,\\,n+1-t)\\in T_n .\n\\]\n\nThese points lie on the hypotenuse of the triangle \\(T_n\\) and are all\ndistinct.  Choose any subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) of size \\(k\\).\nFor every \\(t\\in S\\) define a line \\(L_t\\) that passes through \\(P_t\\) and has\na slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); for instance we may take  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y-(n+1-t)=\\frac12\\bigl(x-t\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nEach such \\(L_t\\) is sunny by construction, and the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{L_t\\mid\nt\\in S\\}\\) are distinct because they pass through distinct points.\n\nFor the remaining indices \\(t\\notin S\\) (there are \\(n-2-k\\) of them) we take\nthe horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_t:\\; y=n+1-t .\n\\]\n\nThese lines are non\u2011sunny and distinct from each other and from \\(H\\) because\ntheir \\(y\\)-coordinates differ.\n\n*Step 3 \u2013 verification of the covering property.*  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) the point lies on \\(V\\) or \\(H\\) respectively.\n* Otherwise \\(a,b\\ge 2\\).  Then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).\n  Hence the point belongs to the horizontal line \\(y=n+1-a\\), which is either\n  one of the lines \\(H_t\\) (if \\(a\\notin S\\)) or, when \\(a\\in S\\), it also lies\n  on the sunny line \\(L_a\\) because \\(L_a\\) passes through the point\n  \\((a,n+1-a)\\) and has slope \\(\\frac12\\neq0\\).  In either case the point is\n  covered.\n\nThus all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered by the constructed family of \\(n\\) lines,\nand exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{L_t\\mid t\\in S\\}\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown\n\n* a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists;\n* no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines (inequality (1));\n* for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration containing\n  exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines can be built.\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
