These are exactly the lattice points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) and \\(a,b\\ge 2\\).\n\n3. **Choice of sunny lines.**  \n   Choose an arbitrary subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with \\(|S|=k\\).\n   For every \\(t\\in S\\) draw the line\n   \\[\n   L_t:\\; y-(n+1-t)=\\frac12\\bigl(x-t\\bigr) .\n   \\]\n   The slope \\(\\frac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each \\(L_t\\) is\n   sunny.  Because the points \\(P_t\\) are distinct, the lines \\(\\{L_t\\mid t\\in S\\}\\)\n   are distinct.\n\n4. **Remaining hypotenuse points.**  \n   For each \\(t\\notin S\\) (there are \\(n-2-k\\) such indices) draw the horizontal\n   line\n   \\[\n   H_t:\\; y=n+1-t .\n   \\]\n   These lines are non‑sunny (they are horizontal) and are distinct from each\n   other and from \\(H\\) because their \\(y\\)-coordinates are different.\n\n5. **The family of \\(n\\) lines.**  \n   The set\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F=\\{V,H\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{L_t\\mid t\\in S\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{H_t\\mid t\\notin S\\}\n   \\]\n   contains exactly \\(2+(n-2)=n\\) lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines\n   \\(L_t\\;(t\\in S)\\) are sunny.\n\n6. **Verification that \\(\\mathcal F\\) covers \\(T_n\\).**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n   *If \\(a=1\\)*, the point lies on \\(V\\).  \n   *If \\(b=1\\)*, the point lies on \\(H\\).  \n\n   *Otherwise \\(a,b\\ge 2\\).*  \n   Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).\n\n   - If \\(b=n+1-a\\) then \\((a,b)=P_a\\).  \n     If \\(a\\in S\\) the point lies on the sunny line \\(L_a\\);  \n     if \\(a\\notin S\\) it lies on the horizontal line \\(H_a\\).\n\n   - If \\(b<n+1-a\\) then \\(b\\) equals some integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-2\\).\n     The horizontal line \\(y=c\\) is either \\(H\\) (when \\(c=1\\)) or one of the\n     lines \\(H_t\\) (when \\(c=n+1-t\\) for a unique \\(t\\notin S\\)).  Hence the point\n     lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n   In every case the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one line of\n   \\(\\mathcal F\\).  Thus \\(\\mathcal F\\) satisfies the covering requirement.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown\n\n* any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines can contain at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines\n  (inequality (1));\n* for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\)\n  distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\)\n  sunny lines.\n\nHence the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a configuration\nexists is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
