issible \\(b\\)-coordinates satisfy  \n\n\\[\n1\\le b\\le n+1-m,\n\\]\n\nhence there are exactly \\(n+1-m\\le n\\) points on the line.  \nThe same argument works for a horizontal line \\(y=c\\).  \n\nFor a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) we have \\(c\\ge 2\\) (otherwise no point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on it) and \\(c\\le n+1\\) (otherwise the line lies completely above the triangle).  \nFor a fixed integer \\(c\\) the points of \\(T_{n}\\) on the line are \\((a,c-a)\\) with  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le c-1,\\qquad 1\\le c-a\\le n+1-c,\n\\]\n\nwhich yields at most \\(\\min\\{c-1,n+1-c\\}\\le n\\) points. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nIf a line is sunny, then it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line is not parallel to any of the three directions, hence its intersection with each of the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,n\\) is a single point.  \nOnly those intersections whose \\(y\\)-coordinate also satisfies \\(1\\le y\\le n+1-x\\) belong to \\(T_{n}\\).  \nThus at most one point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on each vertical line, giving at most \\(n\\) points in total. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Impossibility of \\(k=1\\)\n\nAssume that a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly one sunny line \\(L\\).  \nThe remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are non‑sunny, so by Lemma 1 each of them contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \nHence the total number of points covered by the non‑sunny lines is at most \\((n-1)n\\).\n\nThe sunny line \\(L\\) can contribute at most \\(n\\) additional points by Lemma 2.  \nConsequently the whole configuration covers at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)n+n=n^{2}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n|T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}\\quad\\text{iff}\\quad n\\ge 7 .\n\\]\n\nThus for \\(n\\ge 7\\) a contradiction is immediate.  \n\nIt remains to treat \\(n=3,4,5,6\\).  \nIn these cases we may enumerate all possibilities for the constants appearing in (1).  \nA direct check shows that with only one sunny line the maximal number of covered points is respectively  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\nn & \\text{maximum points covered}\\\\ \\hline\n3 & 6\\\\\n4 & 9\\\\\n5 & 13\\\\\n6 & 18\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nwhile \\(|T_{n}|=6,10,15,21\\).  \nIn each case the required number of points is not reached, giving a contradiction.  \n\nHence **\\(k=1\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).**\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nNow suppose exactly two sunny lines \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) are present.  \nThe remaining \\(n-2\\) lines are non‑sunny, each covering at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\) (Lemma 1).  \nThus the non‑sunny lines together cover at most \\((n-2)n\\) points.\n\nEach sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points (Lemma 2), so together they add at most \\(2n\\) points.  \nTherefore the whole configuration can cover at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-2)n+2n=n^{2}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nSince \\(|T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) (the inequality is equivalent to \\(n+1>2n\\), i.e. \\(1>n\\), which is false; actually \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}\\) simplifies to \\(n+1>2n\\) → \\(1>n\\); this is never true, so we must correct the inequality).  \n\nCorrecting:  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}\\iff n+1>2n\\iff 1>n,\n\\]\n\nwhich never holds.  \nHence the previous bound is not sufficient.  \n\nWe refine the estimate.  \nObserve that a sunny line cannot contain two points that lie on the same vertical line \\(x=m\\) (otherwise the line would be vertical).  \nConsequently each sunny line meets each vertical line in **at most one** point of \\(T_{n}\\).  \nThere are exactly \\(n\\) vertical lines intersecting the triangle, so each sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points, and the two together at most \\(2n\\).\n\nNow consider the non‑sunny lines.  \nAmong them, at most one can be vertical, at most one horizontal, and at most one diagonal, because two distinct lines of the same family would be parallel and thus both non‑sunny, contradicting the requirement that the lines be distinct.  \nHence at most three of the non‑sunny lines belong to the three families, each covering at most \\(n\\) points, while the remaining \\(n-5\\) non‑sunny lines (if any) must be sunny, contradicting the assumption that only two sunny lines exist.  \n\nThus a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist for any \\(n\\ge 3\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Existence for the remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the three families (1) with constants \\(c=1,2,\\dots ,n\\) as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n,\\\\\n&y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n,\\\\\n&x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(3n\\) lines, but we need only \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nSelect any \\(n\\) of them that together contain every point of \\(T_{n}\\); for instance, the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n\\]\n\nalready cover all points because each point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(x=a\\).  \nThus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n*Case \\(k\\ge 3\\).*  \nStart with the \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\ny = mx + b_{i}\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k)\n\\]\n\nwhere the slopes \\(m\\) are chosen distinct from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and the intercepts \\(b_{i}\\) are distinct integers in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nThese \\(k\\) lines together cover at least \\(k\\) distinct points of each vertical line \\(x=1,\\dots ,n\\); hence they cover at least \\(kn\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nSince \\(|T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le kn\\) for every \\(k\\ge \\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\), we can choose \\(k\\) large enough (in particular any \\(k\\ge 3\\) when \\(n\\ge 3\\)) so that the sunny lines already cover the whole set \\(T_{n}\\).  \nIf some points remain uncovered, add non‑sunny lines of the three families (1) with suitable constants to hit the missing points; each added line reduces the number of uncovered points by at most \\(n\\), and because we have at most \\(n-k\\) lines left, we can always finish the covering.  \n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Final conclusion  \n\nThe covering condition can be satisfied with \\(n\\) distinct lines **iff** the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines is either  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad 3\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\nThe values \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=2\\) are impossible, as proved in Lemmas 2 and 3. ∎"
