            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\) define the anti‑diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_t=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=t\\}\n      =\\{(1,t-1),(2,t-2),\\dots ,(t-1,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThere are exactly \\(n-1\\) such sets.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Non‑sunny diagonal covers an anti‑diagonal)\n\nFor every \\(t\\) the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_t:\\;x+y=t\n\\]\n\nis non‑sunny (it is parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\)) and contains\nall points of \\(D_t\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Substituting \\(x+y=t\\) into the definition of \\(D_t\\) gives\n\\(a+b=t\\); thus each point of \\(D_t\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_t\\).\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (A sunny line meets each anti‑diagonal in at most one point)\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line, i.e. its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\nIf \\((a_1,b_1)\\) and \\((a_2,b_2)\\) are two distinct points of \\(T_n\\) lying on\n\\(L\\) and satisfying \\(a_1+b_1=a_2+b_2\\), then the slope of \\(L\\) would be\n\\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of sunny.  Hence \\(L\\) can intersect a\ngiven anti‑diagonal \\(D_t\\) in at most one point. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nChoose a subset  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{t_1,t_2,\\dots ,t_k\\}\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nof size \\(k\\) (if \\(k=n\\) we simply take all \\(n\\) numbers; the construction\nstill works).  \n\nFor each \\(t_i\\in S\\) pick a real number \\(m_i\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nm_i\\notin\\{0,-1\\},\\qquad m_i\\neq m_j\\;(i\\neq j),\n\\]\n\nand also \\(m_i\\neq\\) the slope of any diagonal \\(\\ell_t\\) (all diagonals have\nslope \\(-1\\)).  This is possible because the set \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\\)\nis infinite.\n\nDefine the sunny line  \n\n\\[\ns_i:\\; y=m_i x+(t_i-1-m_i).\n\\]\n\nSince substituting \\(x=1\\) yields \\(y=m_i+(t_i-1-m_i)=t_i-1\\), the point\n\\((1,t_i-1)\\in D_{t_i}\\) lies on \\(s_i\\).  By the choice of the slope,\n\\(s_i\\) is sunny and distinct from all other lines constructed so far.\n\nNow form the following family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) lines:\n\n* for every \\(t\\notin S\\) include the diagonal \\(\\ell_t:x+y=t\\);\n* for every \\(t_i\\in S\\) include the diagonal \\(\\ell_{t_i}\\) **but** we will\n  rely on the sunny line \\(s_i\\) for the point \\((1,t_i-1)\\);\n* finally add the vertical line \\(v:x=1\\).\n\nCounting, we have  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L|=(n-1-|S|)+|S|+1=n .\n\\]\n\nAll lines are distinct: the diagonals have slope \\(-1\\), the vertical line has\nundefined slope, and each sunny line has a slope different from \\(-1,0\\) and\ndifferent from the slopes of the other sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Verification that \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers \\(T_n\\)\n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and let \\(t=a+b\\).\n\n*If \\(t\\notin S\\):* the point belongs to the diagonal \\(\\ell_t\\in\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*If \\(t\\in S\\):*  \n  – If \\((a,b)=(1,t-1)\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(s_i\\) that was\n    constructed for this \\(t\\).  \n  – Otherwise \\((a,b)\\neq(1,t-1)\\) and therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal\n    \\(\\ell_t\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is contained in at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Counting sunny lines\n\nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\) are sunny; all other lines\n(diagonals and the vertical line) are non‑sunny.  Hence \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains\nprecisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Exhaustiveness\n\nSince a family consists of \\(n\\) lines, it cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny\nlines.  The construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.  Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\)\nis exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\text{ are possible}.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
