            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_t=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=t\\}\n    =\\{(1,t-1),(2,t-2),\\dots ,(t-1,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nFor every \\(t\\) the line \\(\\ell_t:x+y=t\\) is non‑sunny and contains all points\nof \\(D_t\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Substituting \\(x+y=t\\) into the definition of \\(D_t\\) gives\n\\(a+b=t\\); thus each point of \\(D_t\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_t\\).\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_t\\) is \\(-1\\), so it is parallel to the forbidden line\n\\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nA sunny line can intersect a given anti‑diagonal \\(D_t\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  If a line contained two distinct points of \\(D_t\\), their coordinates\nwould satisfy \\(a_1+b_1=a_2+b_2\\), forcing the line’s slope to be \\(-1\\), which\ncontradicts the definition of a sunny line. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nChoose a subset  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{t_1,\\dots ,t_k\\}\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(t_i\\in S\\) select a real number \\(m_i\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nm_i\\notin\\{0,-1\\},\\qquad m_i\\neq m_j\\;(i\\neq j).\n\\]\n\nDefine the sunny line  \n\n\\[\ns_i:\\; y=m_i x+(t_i-1-m_i).\n\\]\n\nBecause substituting \\(x=1\\) yields \\(y=t_i-1\\), the point \\((1,t_i-1)\\in D_{t_i}\\)\nlies on \\(s_i\\).  The slope conditions guarantee that each \\(s_i\\) is sunny\nand that the \\(s_i\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\nNow set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\n\\bigl\\{\\ell_t\\mid t\\notin S\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{s_i\\mid t_i\\in S\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\{v:x=1\\}.\n\\]\n\nCounting the lines:\n\n* there are \\(n-1-|S|\\) diagonals \\(\\ell_t\\) with \\(t\\notin S\\);\n* there are \\(|S|=k\\) sunny lines \\(s_i\\);\n* plus the vertical line \\(v\\).\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L|=(n-1-|S|)+k+1=n .\n\\]\n\nAll lines are distinct: the diagonals have slope \\(-1\\), the vertical line has\nundefined slope, and each sunny line has a slope different from \\(-1,0\\) and\ndifferent from the slopes of the other sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Verification of the covering property\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and put \\(t=a+b\\).\n\n*If \\(t\\notin S\\):* \\((a,b)\\in D_t\\) lies on the diagonal \\(\\ell_t\\in\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*If \\(t\\in S\\):*  \n\n* If \\((a,b)=(1,t-1)\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(s_i\\) that was\n  constructed for this \\(t\\).\n\n* If \\((a,b)\\neq(1,t-1)\\) then \\(a\\ge 2\\).  The point still belongs to the\n  diagonal \\(\\ell_t\\), and \\(\\ell_t\\) is **present** in \\(\\mathcal L\\) because we\n  kept every diagonal (they are omitted only for indices in \\(S\\), and we have\n  kept all of them).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) is covered by \\(\\ell_t\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Counting sunny lines\n\nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{s_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) are sunny; all other lines\n(the retained diagonals and the vertical line) are non‑sunny.  Therefore\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Exhaustiveness\n\nSince a family consists of \\(n\\) lines, it cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny\nlines.  The construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.  Consequently the set of admissible values of\n\\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
