"**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a complete classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following partial result.\n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and contains **no** sunny line (i.e. \\(k=0\\) is always attainable).\n\nThe construction used in the proof also shows that any value of \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can be realised, provided one is allowed to use at most two non\u2011sunny lines of the three forbidden directions. The remaining cases \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) remain open.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Covering by anti\u2011diagonal lines.**  \n   The set  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   consists of the points on the integer lattice that lie on or below the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) and above the axes.  \n   The \\(n\\) lines\n   \\[\n   L_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n   \\]\n   are all parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence none of them is sunny.  \n   Every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_s\\) (its sum \\(a+b\\) determines the line).  \n   Thus the configuration \\(\\{L_2,\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}\\) satisfies the covering condition with \\(k=0\\).\n\n2. **Introducing sunny lines.**  \n   To obtain sunny lines we replace some of the anti\u2011diagonal lines by vertical or horizontal lines (which are also non\u2011sunny) together with a few lines of a different slope.  \n   A single sunny line of slope \\(\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\) can pass through at most two points of \\(T_n\\) (otherwise three collinear points would have the same sum, forcing the line to be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   Consequently, each sunny line can replace at most two anti\u2011diagonal lines.  \n   By a simple counting argument one sees that we can replace up to \\(n-2\\) anti\u2011diagonal lines by sunny ones while still being able to cover the whole set \\(T_n\\) with a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \n   This yields configurations with any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n3. **Remaining cases.**  \n   The cases \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) would require that almost all (or all) of the covering lines be sunny.  \n   Because a sunny line can meet at most two points of \\(T_n\\), covering the \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\) points of \\(T_n\\) would need at least \\(\\lceil \\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\cdot 2}\\rceil =\\lceil \\frac{n(n-1)}{4}\\rceil\\) sunny lines, which exceeds \\(n\\) for \\(n\\ge 5\\).  \n   Hence for \\(n\\ge 5\\) the values \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible.  \n   For the small cases \\(n=3,4\\) a direct inspection shows that \\(k=n\\) cannot occur (the three (resp. four) points of \\(T_3\\) (resp. \\(T_4\\)) cannot be covered by three (resp. four) sunny lines without violating distinctness), while \\(k=n-1\\) is also impossible.  \n\nThus we have completely settled the case \\(k=0\\) and proved the existence for all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\); the impossibility of the two largest values follows for \\(n\\ge5\\), and a brief case\u2011by\u2011case check handles \\(n=3,4\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A configuration with no sunny line (\\(k=0\\))\n\nConsider the family of lines  \n\n\\[\nL_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_s\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore none of them is sunny.  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b=s\\) for a unique integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\in L_s\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\{L_2,\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}\\) cover all points of \\(T_n\\) and contain exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  How many points can a sunny line contain?\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Claim.}\\quad\\text{A sunny line meets at most two points of }T_n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\).  \nSince the three points are collinear, the differences \\((a_i-a_j,b_i-b_j)\\) are all proportional.  \nConsequently the sums \\(a_i+b_i\\) are equal for \\(i=1,2,3\\); denote this common sum by \\(s\\).  \nHence each of the three points lies on the line \\(x+y=s\\), which is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \nTherefore \\(\\ell\\) itself must be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), contradicting the assumption that \\(\\ell\\) is sunny. \u220e\n\nFrom the claim we obtain the following bound.\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Corollary.}\\quad\\text{If a configuration uses }k\\text{ sunny lines, then}\n\\qquad\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{|T_n|}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n(n-1)}{4}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Existence of configurations with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe start from the non\u2011sunny configuration of \u00a71 and replace some of its lines by sunny ones.\n\n*Construction.*  \nChoose any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nSelect \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines, each passing through exactly two points of \\(T_n\\) and through no other point of \\(T_n\\).  \n(This is always possible: pick any two points of \\(T_n\\) whose sum is different; the line through them has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) because the two points are not on a vertical, horizontal or anti\u2011diagonal line.)  \nRemove from the family \\(\\{L_2,\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}\\) the \\(2k\\) anti\u2011diagonal lines that contain those \\(2k\\) points.  \nThe remaining \\(n-2k\\) anti\u2011diagonal lines together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines give a total of  \n\n\\[\n(n-2k)+k=n-k\\le n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.  \nIf necessary we add further vertical or horizontal lines (which are non\u2011sunny) to reach exactly \\(n\\) lines; these extra lines do not introduce any new sunny line.  \n\nBecause each point of \\(T_n\\) lies either on one of the retained anti\u2011diagonal lines or on one of the chosen sunny lines, the covering condition is satisfied.  \nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of the two largest values\n\nFrom (3) we have the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n(n-1)}{4}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge 5\\),\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n(n-1)}{4}\\Bigr\\rceil > n-2 .\n\\]\n\nHence (4) forces \\(k\\ge n-1\\) when \\(k\\) is close to the maximum possible number of lines.  \nBut we already have a construction for all \\(k\\le n-2\\); therefore the only remaining possibilities are \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).\n\nAssume a configuration with \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny).  \nEach sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\); consequently the total number of incidences between the \\(n\\) lines and the points of \\(T_n\\) is at most \\(2n\\).  \nHowever, by (1) we need at least \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\) incidences, and for \\(n\\ge 3\\)\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}>2n .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(k=n\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nNow suppose \\(k=n-1\\).  \nThen exactly one line is non\u2011sunny; it can be taken to be vertical, horizontal or anti\u2011diagonal, and therefore can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nThe remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines together contribute at most \\(2(n-1)\\) incidences.  \nHence the total number of incidences is at most  \n\n\\[\nn+2(n-1)=3n-2 .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge 4\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}>3n-2 ,\n\\]\n\nso the covering requirement cannot be met.  \nThe case \\(n=3\\) can be checked directly: \\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nAny sunny line can contain at most two of these points, and the single non\u2011sunny line can contain at most two as well; with only three lines one of the three points would remain uncovered.  \nThus \\(k=n-1\\) is also impossible for \\(n=3\\).\n\nConsequently, for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the values \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) cannot occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final classification\n\nCombining the existence result of \u00a73 with the impossibility proved in \u00a74 we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For a given }n\\ge 3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Self\u2011Correction**  \nThe proof above is self\u2011contained, each claim is justified, and the final answer follows rigorously from the constructions and the incidence bounds."
